WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:07.360
<v Ira Glass>Rebecca remembers exactly when she learned the astonishing truth. She was in second grade and ran to her best friend Rachel at school one day.
<v Ira Glass>丽贝卡清楚地记得自己得知那个惊人真相的时间。那是她上小学二年级的时候，有一天她在学校里朝她的好朋友雷切尔跑去。

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:29.320
<v Rebecca>And she pulled me aside and said, "last night I lost a tooth, and I woke up while the tooth fairy was putting the money under my pillow. And guess who the tooth fairy was." I said, "oh my god, who was it? I have to know." And she said, "my dad. My dad is the tooth fairy."
<v Rebecca>她把我拉到一边说：“昨晚我掉了一颗牙，牙仙把钱塞到我枕头底下时我醒了。你猜牙仙是谁。”我说：“天哪，是谁？我一定要知道。”她说：“我爸爸。我爸爸就是牙仙。”

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:58.230
<v Rebecca>And I remember running home after school and telling my mom, "mom, I know who the tooth fairy is," and declaring it as if I had grown up that I knew who the tooth fairy was. And she said, "oh, well, who is the tooth fairy?" And I turned to her and I said, "Rachel's dad is the tooth fairy. Ronnie Loeberfeld is the tooth fairy."
<v Rebecca>我记得放学后我一路跑回家，对妈妈说：“妈妈，我知道牙仙是谁了，”我信誓旦旦地宣布，仿佛我已经长大成人，知道了牙仙是谁。她说：“哦，是吗，那牙仙是谁？”我转向她说：“雷切尔的爸爸就是牙仙。罗尼·勒伯费尔德就是牙仙。”

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:04.050
<v Rebecca>And she said, "I can't believe you know. It's totally secret. You can't let anyone else know.
<v Rebecca>她说：“真不敢相信你知道了。这绝对是个秘密。你不能让任何人知道。

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:26.860
<v Rebecca>But you're right, Ronnie is the tooth fairy, and he works really hard. And it's a secret, so you can't let anyone else know. He is the tooth fairy, but you can't let anyone else know." And from that day on, Ronnie Loeberfeld was the tooth fairy, and all of my notes under my pillow were signed "Love, Ronnie Loeberfeld."
<v Rebecca>不过你说得对，罗尼就是牙仙，他工作非常努力。这是个秘密，所以你不能让别人知道。他确实是牙仙，但你不能让任何人知道。”从那天起，罗尼·勒伯费尔德就是牙仙，我塞在枕头底下的所有纸条上都署着“爱你的，罗尼·勒伯费尔德”。

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:31.480
<v Ira Glass>Now, in his day job, what did Ronnie Loeberfeld do?
<v Ira Glass>话说回来，罗尼·勒伯费尔德平时是做什么工作的？

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:57.150
<v Rebecca>I think he did something in finance. He was either an accountant or a stockbroker. He worked next to a Stop & Shop in Massachusetts, in Newton. Had dark hair, wore a suit, and I definitely had images of his driving his Volvo around the Boston area and delivering the tooth fairy treats.
<v Rebecca>我想他从事金融方面的工作。他要么是个会计，要么是个股票经纪人。他在马萨诸塞州牛顿市的一家Stop & Shop超市隔壁工作。留着黑头发，穿着西装，我脑海里常常浮现出他开着沃尔沃汽车在波士顿地区四处奔波、分发牙仙礼物的画面。

00:01:57.150 --> 00:02:14.160
<v Rebecca>I remember wondering what it was like for Rachel to know that her dad was the tooth fairy, and definitely being a little envious that her dad had this special job and this special power, and that he had this whole other interesting life, where my dad just came home from work, and that was it.
<v Rebecca>我记得自己当时很好奇，雷切尔知道她爸爸是牙仙是一种什么体验，心里确实也有点羡慕，羡慕她爸爸有这么一份特殊的工作、有着这种特殊的超能力，而且他还有着完全不同且有趣的人生——而我的爸爸下班回家后就什么都没有了。

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:20.010
<v Ira Glass>So when you would actually run into Ronnie Loeberfeld, what was it like for you? How would you act?
<v Ira Glass>所以当你真的偶遇罗尼·勒伯费尔德时，你是什么感觉？你会怎么表现？

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:25.410
<v Rebecca>I tried to act cool. I didn't want to-- you know, it's like if you're starstruck but you don't want them to know that you're star struck?
<v Rebecca>我尽量表现得很酷。我不想——你知道的，就像那种追星族，但又不想让他们知道你是追星族一样？

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:26.790
<v Ira Glass>Just like meeting a celebrity?
<v Ira Glass>就像遇到名人一样？

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:37.560
<v Rebecca>Exactly. You downplay it. You try not to mention it, but you definitely check them out twice and look at them when they walk away. Like, oh my god, you're the tooth fairy.
<v Rebecca>对头。你表现得很低调。你尽量不提这件事，但当他们走开时，你绝对会忍不住回头打量他们两眼。心想，天哪，你就是牙仙。

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:38.830
<v Ira Glass>But you knew enough to play it cool.
<v Ira Glass>但你还算懂得装酷。

00:02:38.830 --> 00:02:49.710
<v Rebecca>I knew enough to play it cool. I said, hey, how are you doing. What's for dinner? How am I getting home tonight? Are my parents going to pick me up if they call?
<v Rebecca>我挺懂装酷的。我说，嘿，你好啊。晚饭吃什么？我今晚怎么回家？如果他们打电话来的话，我爸妈会来接我吗？

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:55.210
<v Ira Glass>You did play it cool.
<v Ira Glass>你确实表现得很酷。

00:02:55.210 --> 00:03:14.320
<v Ira Glass>One interesting question in all this. Why did both girls come to what seems like the least likely conclusion from the evidence in front of them, of a parent swapping money for a tooth under a pillow? Well, Alison Gopnik studies how children think, and she says, of course it's logical for a seven-year-old to conclude that her own father might be the tooth fairy.
<v Ira Glass>这里面有一个有趣的问题。为什么两个女孩都会从眼前的证据——父母把钱和枕头底下的牙齿进行交换——得出看似最不可能的结论呢？艾莉森·戈普尼克专门研究儿童的思考方式，她说，对于一个七岁的孩子来说，推断出她自己的父亲可能就是牙仙，这当然是合乎逻辑的。

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:36.340
<v Alison Gopnik>Children understand that their parents, for instance, are powerful in all sorts of ways that make them very different from children. Now, from a child's point of view, knowing where those powers begin and end is pretty tricky. I mean, think about all the things that your parents can do that you can't do, and think about the fact that there isn't any obvious explanation about why your father can use a Visa card, for instance.
<v Alison Gopnik>孩子们明白，他们的父母在各种方面都很有力量，这使他们与小孩子有很大的不同。然而，从孩子的角度来看，要弄清楚这些力量从哪里开始又到哪里结束是相当棘手的。我是说，想想你父母能做而你不能做的事情，再想想这样一个事实：对于为什么你父亲能使用维萨卡（Visa card）之类的事情，并没有任何显而易见的解释。

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:36.950
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>对。

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:49.780
<v Alison Gopnik>Which is something that you can't do. The power to be a tooth fairy isn't all that much more impressive.
<v Alison Gopnik>这是你做不到的事情。拥有当牙仙的力量其实也没有那么让人觉得高不可攀。

00:03:49.780 --> 00:04:07.870
<v Ira Glass>There's a certain kind of story that kids tell, like the Ronnie Loeberfeld story, where they look at something going on around them, observe it carefully, think about it logically, how one thing connects to the next, and then come to conclusions that are completely incorrect. Therapist Eileen Goldman in Texas tells this story about a little girl on an airplane.
<v Ira Glass>小孩子们经常会讲一种故事，比如罗尼·勒伯费尔德的故事，他们观察周围发生的事情，仔细观察，合乎逻辑地思考一件事是如何与下一件事联系起来的，然后得出完全错误的结论。德克萨斯的治疗师艾琳·戈德曼讲过一个关于飞机上一个小女孩的故事。

00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:31.640
<v Eileen Goldman>And she was about four years old, and her very first flight. And as the plane was airborne, she turned to the woman next to her and said, when do we get smaller? That had been her experience at airports watching airplanes take off-- they do get smaller.
<v Eileen Goldman>她大约四岁，那是她第一次坐飞机。当飞机在空中飞行时，她转过身对旁边的一个女人说：“我们什么时候变小？”这源于她在机场观看飞机起飞的经验——它们确实变小了。

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:51.560
<v Ira Glass>These stories are like jokes, and they're also like poems, I think because there's this "a-ha" quality to them. Some connection is made between things-- a surprising connection, a wrong connection, actually. And part of what makes these so satisfying as stories is that the logic in them is perfectly reasonable and, at the same time, completely and utterly wrong.
<v Ira Glass>这些故事就像笑话，我觉得它们也像诗歌，因为它们有一种“恍然大悟”的特质。事物之间建立起某种联系——一种令人惊奇的联系，其实是一种错误的联系。这些故事之所以如此引人入胜，部分原因在于其中的逻辑既完全合情合理，同时又彻底大错特错。

00:04:51.560 --> 00:05:02.450
<v Ira Glass>We at This American Life love these stories, and so today we bring you a full hour of them. From WBEC Chicago and Public Radio International, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass.
<v Ira Glass>我们美国生活栏目组非常喜欢这些故事，所以今天我们为大家带来整整一小时的此类故事。来自芝加哥WBEC和国际公共广播电台，这里是美国生活。我是艾拉·格拉斯。

00:05:02.450 --> 00:05:17.930
<v Ira Glass>Today on our show, Kid Logic, our show in four acts. Act One, Baby Scientists with Faulty Data. Act Two, The Game Ain't Over Till the Fatso Man Sings. In that act we hear one boy's explanation of how love works.
<v Ira Glass>今天我们的节目是《儿童逻辑》，分为四个幕。第一幕：带着错误数据的婴儿科学家。第二幕：胖子没开口，比赛就没结束。在该幕中，我们将听到一个男孩对爱情运作方式的解释。第三幕：年少时的狼人。迈克尔·沙邦讲述关于成人逻辑与儿童逻辑之间差距的故事。第四幕：一个大脑萎缩，一个大脑长大。一个生病的父亲、他四岁儿子以及《狮子王》中或许并没有什么帮助的人生教训的故事。请继续收听。

00:05:17.930 --> 00:05:45.540
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, Werewolves in Their Youth. That story on the gap between adult and kid logic from Michael Chabon. Act Four, One Brain Shrinks, One Brain Grows. The story of a dad who's sick, his four-year-old boy, and the perhaps not very helpful lessons of The Lion King. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕：带着错误数据的婴儿科学家。

00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:49.200
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Baby Scientists with Faulty Data.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕：带着错误数据的婴儿科学家。

00:05:49.200 --> 00:06:09.460
<v Ira Glass>30 years ago, psychologists and scientists believed that babies could not think at all, that they were irrational and illogical, self-centered little balls of need and want. What science has learned in the last 30 years is that this is not true, that children are observing the world and thinking about it and coming to logical conclusions from the day they're born.
<v Ira Glass>30年前，心理学家和科学家认为婴儿根本不会思考，他们是不理智、不合逻辑、以自我为中心的、充满需求和欲望的小肉球。而科学在过去30年里了解到的是，事实并非如此，孩子们从出生那天起就在观察世界、思考世界，并得出合乎逻辑的结论。

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:31.170
<v Ira Glass>And Alison Gopnik and two of her colleagues decided to summarize a lot of this research in a book. They called it The Scientist in the Crib, meaning that babies are like little scientists. They argue that when a small baby sits in a highchair and drops a spoon onto the floor over and over and over for mom or dad to pick up, what the baby is doing essentially is running a little baby-sized experiment.
<v Ira Glass>艾莉森·戈普尼克和她的两位同事决定将大量这类研究总结成一本书。他们将其命名为《摇篮里的科学家》，意思是婴儿就像小科学家。他们认为，当一个小婴儿坐在高脚椅上，把勺子一次又一次地扔到地上，让爸爸妈妈去捡时，婴儿本质上在做的事就是进行一个婴儿尺度的实验。

00:06:31.170 --> 00:06:51.600
<v Alison Gopnik>Because it turns out that babies are very interested in gravity, and how gravity works. The fact that things fall down and not up is not obvious to babies. And it turns out another thing they're very interested in is human beings and how they work. We are actually the lab rats. They're actually doing experiments on us to see how we tick.
<v Alison Gopnik>因为事实证明，婴儿对重力以及重力是如何运作的非常感兴趣。东西会往下落而不会往上飞，这对婴儿来说并不是显而易见的。事实证明，他们非常感兴趣的另一件事是人类以及人类是如何运作的。我们其实就是实验室里的白鼠。他们实际上在拿我们做实验，以观察我们的运作机制。

00:06:51.600 --> 00:07:08.690
<v Alison Gopnik>So when you play "drop the spoon," you get two for the price of one. You get an experiment about gravity, you get a little physics tutorial, and you get a psychology tutorial. You can see about how that person will do something over and over again.
<v Alison Gopnik>所以当你玩“扔勺子”的游戏时，你花一分钱得到了两份收获。你做了一个关于重力的实验，上了一堂小型的物理课，还上了一堂心理课。你可以观察那个人是如何一遍又一遍地做同一件事的。

00:07:08.690 --> 00:07:28.640
<v Ira Glass>While kids think with the same logic that adults use and apply that logic just as rigorously, there are certain things that they simply do not know and take a while to figure out. Up to six or seven years old, for instance, it's not exactly clear to anyone what is imaginary and what is not, or if wishing for something can make it come true.
<v Ira Glass>虽然孩子们思考时使用的逻辑与成年人相同，并且同样严谨地应用这种逻辑，但有些事情他们就是不知道，需要一段时间才能弄明白。例如，直到六七岁时，任何人对于什么是想象、什么不是想象，或者许愿能否让它成真，都还不是特别清楚。

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:43.190
<v Alison Gopnik>There's a wonderful experiment about this, actually, that Paul Harris in England did, where he got children to imagine that something was in a box. So he would say, OK, now here's this box. We're going to open it up. We're going to close it.
<v Alison Gopnik>英国的保罗·哈里斯其实做过一个非常精彩的实验，他让孩子们想象某个东西在一个盒子里面。所以他会说，好的，现在这里有个盒子。我们要把它打开。我们要把它关上。

00:07:43.190 --> 00:07:56.330
<v Alison Gopnik>Now let's imagine that there's a puppy in this box, or else let's imagine that there's a monster in the box. And he asked the children, is there really a monster in the box? Is there really a puppy in the box? They said, no, they were just imagining it.
<v Alison Gopnik>现在让我们想象这个盒子里有一只小狗，或者让我们想象盒子里有一个怪物。他问孩子们，盒子里真的有怪物吗？盒子里真的有小狗吗？他们回答说，没有，他们只是在想象。

00:07:56.330 --> 00:08:15.730
<v Ira Glass>Then the researcher would walk out of the room, leaving the box behind with the child, and then something funny would happen. The kids who were told to imagine a puppy in the box would go over and peek inside the box, just to check. And the kids who were told that there was a monster in the box, they would edge away from the box.
<v Ira Glass>然后研究人员会走出房间，把盒子留在孩子身边，接着有趣的事情就会发生。被告知盒子里有一只小狗的孩子会走过去往盒子里偷看，只是为了核对一下。而那些被告知盒子里有怪物的孩子，则会默默挪开，远离盒子。

00:08:15.730 --> 00:08:36.820
<v Alison Gopnik>So they weren't going to take any chances, just in case wishing actually could make monsters happen. They didn't want to take any chances about what was going on in that box. But by the time the children are six or seven, like grownups, they've understood that just wishing for things isn't going to actually make them happen.
<v Alison Gopnik>所以他们不会去冒任何风险，以防万一许愿真的能让怪物成真。他们不想对那个盒子里面发生的事情冒任何风险。但到了六七岁的时候，孩子们和成年人一样，已经明白单纯地许愿并不能让事情真正发生。

00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:52.340
<v Ira Glass>When they're still small and inexperienced about what happens in the real world, children have to make logical inferences all the time based on the data that they do have. Here is how children responded when our producer Jonathan Goldstein asked them about the tooth fairy.
<v Ira Glass>当孩子们还很小、对真实世界中发生的事情缺乏经验时，他们必须始终根据自己拥有的数据做出逻辑推论。当我们的制作人乔纳森·戈德斯坦向孩子们询问关于牙仙的问题时，他们的回答是这样的。

00:08:52.340 --> 00:08:56.470
<v Jonathan Goldstein>What do you think she does with all of these teeth that she's collecting?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>你认为她收集了这么多牙齿都用来做什么？

00:08:56.470 --> 00:08:58.990
<v Boy>Maybe she gives it to the people without teeth.
<v Boy>也许她把牙齿送给没有牙齿的人。

00:08:58.990 --> 00:09:00.410
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Like who?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>比如谁？

00:09:00.410 --> 00:09:02.080
<v Boy>Old people.
<v Boy>老年人。

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:04.690
<v Jonathan Goldstein>What do you think she does with all these teeth?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>你觉得她收集这些牙齿用来做什么？

00:09:04.690 --> 00:09:08.450
<v Boy>I really think she just likes to collect teeth and make things out of them.
<v Boy>我真的觉得她只是喜欢收集牙齿，并用它们做东西。

00:09:08.450 --> 00:09:10.430
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Like what kind of stuff?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>比如做什么样的东西？

00:09:10.430 --> 00:09:19.570
<v Boy>Lots of stuff. Makes a tooth house, a tooth trophy, and a tooth desk.
<v Boy>很多东西。做一个牙齿房子、一个牙齿奖杯和一个牙齿书桌。

00:09:19.570 --> 00:09:22.760
<v Jonathan Goldstein>How many teeth do you think it takes to make a tooth house?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>你觉得盖一栋牙齿房子需要多少颗牙齿？

00:09:22.760 --> 00:09:24.490
<v Boy>100.
<v Boy>（笑声）

00:09:24.490 --> 00:09:25.690
<v Girl>100.
<v Girl>（笑声）

00:09:25.690 --> 00:09:29.920
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Why wouldn't she just make the house out of bricks like everyone else?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>她为什么不像其他人一样用砖头造房子呢？

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:34.540
<v Girl>Because I don't-- because no one doesn't have brick teeth.
<v Girl>因为我不知道——因为没有人有砖头牙齿。

00:09:34.540 --> 00:09:43.210
<v Girl>[MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Girl>[音乐播放]

00:09:43.210 --> 00:10:05.140
<v Ira Glass>These stories where kids take a perfectly logical premise and go through a series of perfectly logical deductions that lead to perfectly incorrect conclusions. Turns out that science does not have a name for these stories, which is surprising, given how common they are and how they are recognized around the world for their sheer entertainment value. Here, we've collected a few more.
<v Ira Glass>在这些故事中，孩子们采取一个完全合乎逻辑的前提，然后通过一系列完全合乎逻辑的推论，最终得出完全错误的结论。事实证明，科学界对这类故事还没有一个统一的称呼，考虑到它们是如此普遍，并且因其纯粹的娱乐价值而在世界各地广受认可，这确实令人惊奇。在这里，我们收集了更多此类故事。

00:10:05.140 --> 00:10:19.840
<v Woman>We lived in a duplex. The duplexes directly to the left and the right of use were aunts and uncles, across the street from us, all aunts and uncles, so there was no such thing as walking out and seeing a stranger. I just thought we all looked alike, we all had common ancestries.
<v Woman>我们住在一栋复式公寓里。紧挨着我们左右两边的复式公寓里住着叔叔阿姨，街对面也全是叔叔阿姨，所以根本不可能出门遇到陌生人。我当时以为我们长得都一样，我们都有着共同的祖先。

00:10:19.840 --> 00:10:42.890
<v Woman>Well, when I became mobile, when I got my first tricycle, I could go a little bit further, so I ventured down the street. And I looked, and I saw this couple sitting there, these two people, but there were people that I had never seen before. I'd never seen anything like that, because they were white people. And because I had never seen white people, I assumed that they were ghosts.
<v Woman>嗯，当我开始能够走动、有了第一辆三轮车时，我就可以走得稍微远一点了，于是我冒险走到了街上。我抬头一看，看到一对夫妇坐在那里，这两个人是我从未见过的人。我从未见过这样的情景，因为他们是白人。因为我从来没见过白人，所以我以为他们是鬼魂。

00:10:42.890 --> 00:10:48.310
<v Woman>So I waved, like, I wonder if I wave what kind of people are they? What do they do? Do they talk?
<v Woman>所以我挥了挥手，心想，不知道如果我挥手，他们是什么样的人？他们会做什么？他们会说话吗？

00:10:48.310 --> 00:11:19.180
<v Woman>So I waved, and I remember hearing the man going [HACKING COUGH] and I thought, wow, that must be the way they talk. It was like a scientific discovery-- like, I discovered the first ghost people and they talked to me. I communicated. I waved, they waved. I said hello, and they said hello in their language. [COUGHING]
<v Woman>所以我挥了挥手，我记得听到那个男人发出[清嗓子的咳嗽声]，我想，哇，他们说话一定是这种方式。这就像是一个科学发现——就像我发现了第一批鬼魂，而且他们跟我说话了。我进行了交流。我挥手，他们挥手。我说了你好，他们也用他们的语言说了你好。[咳嗽声]

00:11:19.180 --> 00:11:51.540
<v Jack Hitt>Well, it all began at Christmas two years ago when my daughter was four years old. And it was the first time that she had ever asked about what did this holiday mean. And so I explained to her that this was celebrating the birth of Jesus. And she wanted to know more about that, and we went out and bought a kid's Bible and had these readings at night. She loved them, wanted to know everything about Jesus.
<v Jack Hitt>这一切始于两年前的圣诞节，当时我女儿四岁。那是她第一次询问这个节日意味着什么。于是我向她解释，这是为了庆祝耶稣的诞生。她想了解更多这方面的信息，于是我们出去买了一本儿童圣经，晚上读这些内容。她很喜欢，想了解关于耶稣的一切。

00:11:51.540 --> 00:12:11.550
<v Jack Hitt>So we read a lot about his birth and about his teaching. And she would ask constantly what that phrase was, and I would explain to her that it was "do unto others as you would have them do unto you." And we would talk about those old words and what that all meant.
<v Jack Hitt>所以我们读了很多关于他的诞生和他的教导的内容。她会经常问那句话是什么，我就会向她解释那是“你想别人怎样对待你，你也要怎样对待别人”。我们会讨论那些古老的词语及其背后的含义。

00:12:11.550 --> 00:12:57.040
<v Jack Hitt>And then one day we were driving past a big church, and out front was an enormous crucifix. She said, who is that? And I guess I'd never really told that part of the story. [LAUGHS] So I had to sort of, "yeah, well, that's Jesus, and I forgot to tell you the ending. Yeah, well, he ran afoul of the Roman government. This message that he had was so radical and unnerving to the prevailing authorities at the time that they had to kill him. They came to the conclusion that he would have to die. That message was too troublesome."
<v Jack Hitt>然后有一天，我们开车经过一座大教堂，正门前方立着一个巨大的耶稣受难像。她问：“那是谁？”我想我从来没有真正讲过故事的那一部分。[笑] 所以我只好打马虎眼说：“哦，嗯，那是耶稣，我忘了告诉你结局了。嗯，他触犯了罗马政府。他带来的信息在当时的当权者看来是如此激进和令人不安，以至于他们不得不杀了他。他们得出的结论是，他必须死。那个信息太麻烦了。”

00:12:57.040 --> 00:13:19.780
<v Jack Hitt>It was about a month later after that Christmas, we had gone through the whole story of what Christmas meant. And it was mid-January, and her preschool celebrates the same holidays as the local schools, so Martin Luther King Day was off. So I knocked off work that day, and I decided we'd play and I'd take her out to lunch.
<v Jack Hitt>大约在那次圣诞节的一个月后，我们已经把圣诞节的含义整个讲了一遍。那是1月中旬，她的幼儿园放假安排和当地学校一样，所以马丁·路德·金纪念日那天不用上学。那天我便放下手头的工作，决定陪她玩，带她出去吃午饭。

00:13:19.780 --> 00:13:51.610
<v Jack Hitt>And we were sitting in there, and right on the table where we happened to plop down was the art section of the local newspaper, and there, big-as-life, was a huge drawing by a 10-year-old kid in the local schools of Martin Luther King. And she said, "who's that?" And I said, "Well, as it happens, that's Martin Luther King, and he's why you're not in school today. So we're celebrating his birthday. This is the day we celebrate his life."
<v Jack Hitt>我们坐在那儿，就在我们恰好坐下的桌子上，摆着当地报纸的艺术版块，上面有一个当地学校10岁孩子画的马丁·路德·金的巨大画像，画得跟真人一样大。她问：“那是谁？”我说：“哦，真巧，那是马丁·路德·金，这就是你今天不用上学的原因。所以我们在庆祝他的生日。这是我们庆祝他一生的日子。”

00:13:51.610 --> 00:14:11.830
<v Jack Hitt>And she said, "so who is he?" I said, "well, he was a preacher." And she looks up at me and goes, "for Jesus?" And I said, "yeah, actually he was. But there was another thing that he was really famous for, which is that he had a message."
<v Jack Hitt>她问：“那他是谁？”我说：“哦，他是个牧师。”她抬起头看着我问：“是为耶稣吗？”我说：“对，其实是的。但他还有另一件事非常出名，那就是他有一种精神/他传递了一种信息。”

00:14:11.830 --> 00:14:40.520
<v Jack Hitt>And you're trying to say this to a four-year-old. This is the first time they ever hear anything, so you're just very careful about how you phrase everything. So I said, well, yeah, he was a preacher and he had a message. And she said, "what was his message?" I said, "well, he said that you should treat everybody the same, no matter what they look like."
<v Jack Hitt>你要对一个四岁的孩子说这些。这是他们第一次听到任何此类事情，所以你必须非常小心措辞。所以我说，嗯，是的，他是一个牧师，他有一种精神/信息。她问：“他的信息是什么？”我说：“嗯，他说过，无论人们长什么样，你都应该平等对待每个人。”

00:14:40.520 --> 00:15:15.710
<v Jack Hitt>She thought about that for a minute, and she said, "well, that's what Jesus said." I said, "yeah, I guess it is. I never thought of it that way, but yeah, that is sort of like "do unto others as you would have them do unto you." And she thought for a minute and looked up at me and said, "did they kill him, too?"
<v Jack Hitt>她思考了一分钟，然后说：“嗯，耶稣也是这么说的。”我说：“对，我想是的。我从来没从这个角度想过，不过是的，这确实有点像‘你想别人怎样对待你，你也要怎样对待别人’。”她想了一会儿，抬头看着我问：“他们也把他杀了吗？”

00:15:15.710 --> 00:15:20.180
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, The Game Ain't Over Till The Fatso Man Sings.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕：胖子没开口，比赛就没结束。

00:15:20.180 --> 00:15:35.970
<v Ira Glass>When little kids talk about a crush or love, are they talking about more or less the same thing that adults mean by those words? Well, Howie Chackowicz remembers how he thought about love in grade school. He wanted girls to like him, but they never seemed to. Here he is.
<v Ira Glass>当小孩子们谈论暗恋或爱情时，他们谈论的东西和成年人所指的那些词基本上是一回事吗？嗯，豪伊·查科维奇还记得自己在小学时是怎么看待爱情的。他想让女孩子们喜欢他，但她们似乎从来没喜欢过他。他这就来了。

00:15:35.970 --> 00:15:51.580
<v Howie Chackowicz>Looking back on it, I think part of the problem was how I thought about love as a kid. I had a few ideas about how you get someone to love you that, in retrospect, weren't particularly helpful to me. First, I thought that if they can see me sleeping, they would immediately fall for me.
<v Howie Chackowicz>回想起来，我觉得部分问题在于我小时候对爱情的看法。我当时对如何让别人爱上有了一些想法，现在回想起来，对我并没有什么大用。首先，我以为只要她们看到我睡觉的样子，就会立刻爱上我。

00:15:51.580 --> 00:16:30.490
<v Howie Chackowicz>When I went to sleep each night, I would consciously try to sleep in a cute way, just in case the girls I like would peep on me. I'd roll into a fetal ball like a kitten and scrunch my head into my pillow, hand under my head. I imagined all the popular girls intent on cruelly pranking me got a ladder and climbed up to my bedroom window. But instead of painting "fatso" or whatever on my window as planned, their collective hearts would melt as they saw me sleeping like a babe, an angel buried snugly under my blankets. I guess it was some crossover of a kid's knowledge of what was endearing to adults applied to romance.
<v Howie Chackowicz>每天晚上睡觉时，我都会刻意摆出一个可爱的睡姿，以防我喜欢的女孩偷看我。我会像小猫一样蜷缩成一团，把脑袋塞进枕头里，手垫在头下方。我幻想着所有那些一心想对进行残酷恶作剧的受欢迎的女孩会拿来梯子爬到我的卧室窗户上。但她们没有像计划的那样在我的窗户上涂写“大胖子”之类的侮辱字眼，而是看到我像个婴儿、像一个裹在毯子里安睡的天使一样睡觉时，她们内心深处的冰山瞬间融化了。我想这大概是把小孩子对成年人觉得什么是可爱的事物的理解，生搬硬套到了浪漫爱情上。

00:16:30.490 --> 00:16:44.800
<v Howie Chackowicz>My second theory was that they'd fall in love with me if they could see me reading aloud. This conclusion came out of my experience with nieces and nephews who would fawn all over me when I would read to them. By age six, I was already an uncle, and I felt this lent me a certain maturity.
<v Howie Chackowicz>我的第二个理论是，如果她们能看到我大声朗读，就会爱上我。这个结论来自于我的经验，当我给侄子侄女们读故事时，他们会非常崇拜我。我六岁时就已经当了叔叔，我觉得这赋予了我某种成熟感。

00:16:44.800 --> 00:16:59.770
<v Howie Chackowicz>Often, at recess time, I'd go to the back of the classroom and read from a selection of kids books. All the kids would gather round in a circle. I'd pore through books like "Percy, the Rose-Eating Donkey," affecting the voices of the different characters and speaking with a preacher's sweaty charisma.
<v Howie Chackowicz>通常，在课间休息时，我会走到教室后面，从精选的儿童读物里挑书读。所有的小朋友都会围成一圈。我会仔细研读像《玫瑰食驴珀西》这样的书，模仿不同角色的声音，并带着传教士那种大汗淋漓的个人魅力说话。

00:16:59.770 --> 00:17:24.060
<v Howie Chackowicz>I'm not sure why, but everyone in my class seemed to love the way I hammed it up. The only problem with this was, the girls in class ended up treating me like their uncle. They called me "Uncle Howie" and talk to me in baby talk. [BABY TALK] "Wead me a stowwie, Uncle Howie," so on. Don't get me wrong. I loved the attention, but I wanted love, not "wuv."
<v Howie Chackowicz>我不知道为什么，但班上的每个人似乎都很喜欢我这种夸张的表演。这唯一的麻烦在于，班上的女孩子们最后把我当成了她们的叔叔。她们叫我“豪伊叔叔”，还用娃娃腔跟我说话。[娃娃腔]“给我读个故事吧，豪伊叔叔，”诸如此类。别误会我。我很享受这种关注，但我想要的是爱情，而不是“宝宝爱”。

00:17:24.060 --> 00:17:40.740
<v Howie Chackowicz>So I had all these ideas about love. And of all the girls I knew, my theories were most intensely targeted at one girl, the most popular girl in school, Karen. She became my most serious crush. I carried a torch for Karen from grade one to grade six.
<v Howie Chackowicz>所以我当时对爱情有各种各样的想法。而在我认识的所有女孩中，我的这些理论最强烈地针对着一个女孩——学校里最受欢迎的女孩凯伦。她成了我最认真的暗恋对象。从一年级到六年级，我对凯伦一直念念不忘。

00:17:40.740 --> 00:18:07.960
<v Howie Chackowicz>Though Karen didn't seem to like me much, one thing I learned about love on TV was that if one was sincere, love can break all boundaries. I believed that there would come a moment where I'd speak the words "I love you" to Karen with such tenderness and tears that it would break her heart and she would cry, too, and confess her love. I would allow one brave tear to travel down my cheek.
<v Howie Chackowicz>尽管凯伦似乎并不怎么喜欢我，但我从电视上学到关于爱情的一点是，只要一个人足够真诚，爱就能打破所有的界限。我相信总会有那么一个时刻，我会对凯伦说出“我爱你”这三个字，语气饱含温柔与泪水，足以让她心碎，然后她也会哭泣并向我表白。我会允许一滴勇敢的眼泪顺着我的脸颊滑落。

00:18:07.960 --> 00:18:08.740
<v Howie Chackowicz>And I was really cute then.
<v Howie Chackowicz>我那时候真的很可爱。

00:18:08.740 --> 00:18:09.240
<v Karen>Yeah.
<v Karen>是啊。

00:18:09.100 --> 00:18:09.970
<v Howie Chackowicz>And Jonathan looked like cute Jonathan.
<v Howie Chackowicz>乔纳森看起来就像可爱的乔纳森。

00:18:09.970 --> 00:18:15.610
<v Karen>And Jonathan is very, very adorable.
<v Karen>而乔纳森非常非常招人喜爱。

00:18:15.610 --> 00:18:36.270
<v Howie Chackowicz>Now, years later, I'm friends with Karen-- actual friends with both her and her husband, Alan. I even worked for him for a while. Karen and I have talked before about our elementary school days, usually steering the conversation towards how mean she was to me, but I've never really spoken with her about puppy love. I wanted to know what she remembered, whether she knew I even had a crush on her at all.
<v Howie Chackowicz>如今，多年以后，我和凯伦成了朋友——我和她以及她的丈夫阿兰都成了真正的朋友。我甚至还为他工作过一段时间。我和凯伦以前聊过我们的小学时光，通常谈话的主题都会转到她以前对我是多么刻薄，但我从来没有真正和她聊过青梅竹马的纯纯爱恋。我想知道她还记得什么，甚至想知道她当年是否知道我暗恋过她。

00:18:36.270 --> 00:18:40.350
<v Howie Chackowicz>Who were your interests? We'll go year-by-year.
<v Howie Chackowicz>你当时对谁感兴趣？我们按年份来数。

00:18:40.350 --> 00:18:48.510
<v Karen>Keith, definitely, love interest. Barry Seller, the big one. The big one.
<v Karen>基斯，绝对算，暗恋对象。巴里·塞勒，一个重量级的。重量级的。

00:18:48.510 --> 00:18:49.860
<v Howie Chackowicz>Loren Wilter?
<v Howie Chackowicz>洛伦·威尔特？

00:18:49.860 --> 00:18:50.720
<v Karen>Yes, yes!
<v Karen>对，对！

00:18:50.720 --> 00:18:51.220
<v Howie Chackowicz>Yes.
<v Howie Chackowicz>对。

00:18:51.080 --> 00:18:52.170
<v Karen>Yes.
<v Karen>对。

00:18:52.170 --> 00:19:08.910
<v Howie Chackowicz>Notice who she doesn't mention. Even though it was so far in the past, that crush is still such an awkward thing to talk about. When I finally did tell her about how I felt about her when we were kids, I sort of mumbled my way through it, backpedaling all over the place. I even forgot to actually point the mic at her.
<v Howie Chackowicz>注意她没有提到谁。尽管事情已经过去那么久，但谈起那段暗恋依然是一件非常尴尬的事。当我终于向她坦白我们小时候我对她的真实感受时，我支支吾吾地把话说完，一路上不停地打退堂鼓。我甚至忘了把话筒对准她。

00:19:08.910 --> 00:19:12.060
<v Howie Chackowicz>When I was in elementary school, you were like a big crush.
<v Howie Chackowicz>我上小学的时候，你就像是一个很大的暗恋对象。

00:19:12.060 --> 00:19:12.750
<v Karen>Aww.
<v Karen>噢。

00:19:12.750 --> 00:19:13.350
<v Howie Chackowicz>You see?
<v Howie Chackowicz>你看？

00:19:13.350 --> 00:19:34.420
<v Karen>I didn't even know that. I thought I was just responsible for tormenting you. I didn't realize that there was a crush involved. Maybe at the time I knew, but I had no clue, actually, that you liked me when I think back on it now.
<v Karen>我当时根本不知道。我以为我只负责折磨你。现在回想起来，我根本不知道这里面还有暗恋这回事。也许当时我知道，但实际上我一点头绪也没有，原来你那时候喜欢我。

00:19:34.420 --> 00:19:40.930
<v Howie Chackowicz>One time in the field, Keith told me to tackle you. He said, if you tackle her, she'll like you, and then you'll be popular.
<v Howie Chackowicz>有一次在操场上，基斯让我タック你。他说，如果你タック她，她就会喜欢你，然后你就会受欢迎。

00:19:40.930 --> 00:19:41.830
<v Karen>That is so--
<v Karen>这太——

00:19:41.830 --> 00:19:42.330
<v Howie Chackowicz>This is the thing.
<v Howie Chackowicz>就是这样。

00:19:42.230 --> 00:19:42.950
<v Karen>--funny.
<v Karen>——太搞笑了。

00:19:42.950 --> 00:19:43.690
<v Howie Chackowicz>So the story goes--
<v Howie Chackowicz>所以故事是这样的——

00:19:43.690 --> 00:19:45.820
<v Karen>You know what, that's very good advice, I think.
<v Karen>你知道吗，我觉得这真是个好建议。

00:19:45.820 --> 00:19:56.710
<v Howie Chackowicz>It was very bad advice. I would have broke your leg. Basically what happened was, I was standing off to the sidelines. I wasn't often picked to play. This was a coed game and seemed like very fun.
<v Howie Chackowicz>这是个非常糟糕的建议。我当时会把你的腿弄断的。基本上当时的情况是，我站在场边。我不太常被选去玩。这是一场男女混合游戏，看起来非常有趣。

00:19:56.710 --> 00:20:08.320
<v Howie Chackowicz>And Keith said, "I'm going to throw Karen the ball." And he goes-- you know he couldn't pronounce his "Rs"-- he goes, "you tackle her, Howie. Tackle her, tackle her hard and you'll be popular, and then everyone will like you." I'm like, "OK, I'm going to do it."
<v Howie Chackowicz>基斯说：“我要把球传给凯伦。”然后他说——你知道他发不出“R”的音——他说，“你去タック她，豪伊。タック她，狠狠地タック她，你就会受欢迎，然后大家都会喜欢你。”我说：“好吧，我要上了。”

00:20:08.320 --> 00:20:28.300
<v Howie Chackowicz>And I remember you were running, the sun is shining off and your hair's bouncing, and you caught the ball. And I remember I just-- I don't know what came over me. I just remember thinking, like, that's what I had to do, was I had to tackle you, and I tackled you really hard. You're on the ground. You're holding your leg. Now, any kind of logic would have dictated that's not the way to get the girl you like, right?
<v Howie Chackowicz>我记得你当时在奔跑，阳光洒下来，你的头发在一颤一颤的，你接住了球。我记得我当时就——我不知道自己是怎么了。我只记得当时想，这就是我必须做的事，我必须タック你，而且我狠狠地把你タック倒了。你倒在地上。你捂着腿。现在看来，任何逻辑都会表明这不是追求喜欢的女孩的方法，对吧？

00:20:28.300 --> 00:20:39.370
<v Karen>Yeah, but a lot of times the way young kids react, or show affection, is through physical-- like I was telling you before, that I wrestled with Barry, because--
<v Karen>对，但很多时候，小孩子的反应或表达喜爱的方式是通过肢体上的——就像我刚才跟你说的，我和巴里摔跤，因为——

00:20:39.370 --> 00:20:40.000
<v Howie Chackowicz>That's right.
<v Howie Chackowicz>没错。

00:20:40.000 --> 00:20:41.530
<v Karen>--you just want to be close.
<v Karen>——你只是想离得近一点。

00:20:41.530 --> 00:21:02.090
<v Howie Chackowicz>This is not how she felt about it at the time, because I felt the harder I tackled her, the more popular I'd be. I took her down like it was prison football. The game came to an immediate end, everyone circling Karen's writhing body, the football near her lay totally still. She was holding her leg, looking up at me, saying, "you tub of lard, you broke my leg!"
<v Howie Chackowicz>当时她可不这么觉得，因为我觉得我タック得越狠，就会越受欢迎。我把她放倒的架势就像是在监狱里踢橄榄球。比赛当场宣告结束，所有人都围着凯伦痛苦扭动的身体，橄榄球就在她身旁一动不动。她捂着腿，抬头看着我说：“你这个大肥猪，你踢断我的腿了！”

00:21:02.090 --> 00:21:36.540
<v Howie Chackowicz>Karen doesn't remember any of this. She doesn't remember how she then jumped up, got four or five of her girlfriends in the huddle, and miraculously choreographed an impromptu kicking chorus line of "Fatso Men" to the tune of the Village People's "Macho Men." Fatso, fatso men, I would not like to be a fatso man. Fatso, fatso men, I would not like to be a fatso. At that point, they all through their hands up in the air in unison.
<v Howie Chackowicz>凯伦对这一切毫无记忆。她不记得自己当时是如何一跃而起，把四五个女性朋友拉进圈子里，然后奇迹般地即兴排练了一支踢踏舞合唱队，伴着乡村村民合唱团的《肌肉男》的曲调跳起“肥猪男”之歌。肥猪，肥猪男，我可不想当个肥猪男。肥猪，肥猪男，我可不想当个肥猪。说到这儿，她们齐刷刷地把双手高高举过头顶。

00:21:36.540 --> 00:21:54.240
<v Howie Chackowicz>I remember it so perfectly, but then, after all, it was my crush. She had no recollection of the time the school photographer called her Daisy Duke and then turned around and called me Boss Hogg. Or the fitness day that I beat her in a "Chariots of Fire" style race. She didn't even remember the biggest story of them all-- our sixth grade graduation dance.
<v Howie Chackowicz>我把这一切记得清清楚楚，但毕竟，那是我暗恋的对象嘛。她完全不记得学校摄影师叫她黛西·杜克，然后转过头来叫我波斯·霍格。她也不记得那次健身日，我在一场模仿《烈火战车》风格的赛跑中跑赢了她。她甚至不记得所有故事中最盛大的一个——我们的六年级毕业舞会。

00:21:54.240 --> 00:21:58.920
<v Howie Chackowicz>Now, the last dance was "Stairway to Heaven," right? Now, I went to the dance to dance with you.
<v Howie Chackowicz>话说最后一支舞是《通往天国的阶梯》，对吧？当时我去舞会就是为了跟你跳舞。

00:21:58.920 --> 00:21:59.550
<v Karen>Aww.
<v Karen>噢。

00:21:59.550 --> 00:22:10.560
<v Howie Chackowicz>But I couldn't, because as I was walking, a line of people walked by and blocked me. And then-- it was like a split second-- but then you're in the arms of a grade seven.
<v Howie Chackowicz>但我没能跳成，因为当我走过去的时候，一排人走过来挡住了我。然后——就像是一眨眼的工夫——你就落入了一个七年级男生的怀抱。

00:22:10.560 --> 00:22:12.570
<v Karen>Really? What was a grade seven doing in our--
<v Karen>真的吗？一个七年级生怎么会来我们的——

00:22:12.570 --> 00:22:15.150
<v Howie Chackowicz>They crashed our grade six graduation dance.
<v Howie Chackowicz>他们闯入了我们的六年级毕业舞会。

00:22:15.150 --> 00:22:16.260
<v Karen>Who was it? I don't even remember.
<v Karen>那是谁？我根本不记得了。

00:22:16.260 --> 00:22:22.770
<v Howie Chackowicz>I don't know. He was tall and thin, he had longish hair, and he came through the back door of the gym. You don't remember the last dance?
<v Howie Chackowicz>我不知道。他又高又瘦，留着长头发，从体育馆的后门走了进来。你不记得最后一支舞了吗？

00:22:22.770 --> 00:22:23.700
<v Karen>No.
<v Karen>不记得。

00:22:23.700 --> 00:22:25.230
<v Howie Chackowicz>You kissed this gentleman.
<v Howie Chackowicz>你吻了这个绅士。

00:22:25.230 --> 00:22:26.850
<v Howie Chackowicz>[LAUGHS]
<v Howie Chackowicz>[笑声]

00:22:26.850 --> 00:22:33.270
<v Karen>Did I? Like a peck or a make-out kiss? Because I don't think I was making out in grade six.
<v Karen>我有吗？只是轻轻一吻还是法式热吻？因为我觉得我在六年级时不可能跟人法式热吻。

00:22:33.270 --> 00:22:35.400
<v Howie Chackowicz>No. By adult standards, it was a peck.
<v Howie Chackowicz>没有。按成年人的标准，这就是轻轻一吻。

00:22:35.400 --> 00:22:37.470
<v Howie Chackowicz>[LAUGHS]
<v Howie Chackowicz>[笑声]

00:22:37.470 --> 00:22:48.930
<v Howie Chackowicz>I'd say by grade six standards, you got laid.
<v Howie Chackowicz>我觉得按六年级的标准，你已经算“失身”了。

00:22:48.930 --> 00:22:52.110
<v Ira Glass>It turns out that Karen remembered exactly one story about me.
<v Ira Glass>事实证明，凯伦只记得关于我的一个故事。

00:22:52.110 --> 00:23:32.970
<v Karen>Well, my most vivid memory of you is sitting in class and the teacher asking us to pull out our homework. And you [LAUGHS] opening up your desk, and the paper kind of overflowing out of that desk, and you rummaging frantically through the desk trying to find what homework we were asked to take out, and not being able to find it. And our teacher walking up to your desk and everyone knowing what was coming, because it probably happened two days before, and the teacher just lacing into you and dumping the contents of your desk on the floor. [LAUGHS]
<v Karen>嗯，我对你最生动的记忆是，当时我们坐在教室里，老师让我们拿出家庭作业。而你[笑声]打开你的课桌，纸张简直从那个课桌里溢了出来，你拼命地在课桌里翻找老师要求拿出的作业，却怎么也找不到。接着我们的老师走到你的课桌前，所有人都知道接下来要发生什么了，因为这可能两天前刚发生过，老师随即把你一顿痛斥，把你的课桌里的东西哗啦一下全倒在了地板上。[笑声]

00:23:32.970 --> 00:23:36.440
<v Howie Chackowicz>Now, when that happened, did I seem cool, like a bad boy?
<v Howie Chackowicz>话说当时发生这事时，我看起来很酷、像个坏男孩吗？

00:23:36.440 --> 00:23:49.950
<v Karen>[LAUGHS] No. Everyone felt very, very sad for you.
<v Karen>[笑声]没有。每个人都觉得你非常非常可怜。

00:23:49.950 --> 00:24:06.430
<v Howie Chackowicz>More than anything, I wanted Karen to notice me, but not in that way. I think problem with my theories was that I expected her to fall for me the same way I fell for her. That she would see me from afar, reading to our classmates, sleeping like a little prince. I thought that's what it took for someone to fall in love.
<v Howie Chackowicz>比起什么都重要，我希望凯伦注意到我，但不是以那种方式。我觉得我的理论的问题在于，我期望她爱上我的方式和我爱上她的方式一模一样。我希望她能远远地看到我，给同学们朗读，像个小王子一样安睡。我以为这就是让人坠入爱河所需要的全部。

00:24:06.430 --> 00:24:27.770
<v Howie Chackowicz>I wanted her to think that this was the real me. I wanted to think it was the real me. And the truth of it was that the real, real me was getting screamed at and having his desk spilled out on the ground each day.
<v Howie Chackowicz>我希望她认为这才是真实的我。我也希望自己能认为这就是真实的我。而事实的真相是，真实、真正的我每天都在挨训，课桌里的东西每天都被倒在地上。

00:24:27.770 --> 00:24:54.890
<v Howie Chackowicz>There's a way you can love a girl in grade six that you'll never have again. There's something about kids, or at least the way I was as a kid, that is purely romantic in the truest love salutatorian sense of the word. Only a year or two later, my theories in the ways of love had changed drastically. By seventh grade, I had some spin the bottle sessions under my belt and I concluded that instead of dreaming about a true love I couldn't have, I should get a little bit more pragmatic about the whole thing.
<v Howie Chackowicz>人在六年级时对一个女孩的爱，是你以后再也不会拥有的。小孩子身上有一种特质，或者至少我小时候那种特质，在最纯正的恋爱致辞意义上是纯粹浪漫的。仅仅一两年后，我对爱情之道的理论就发生了巨变。到了七年级，我已经经历过几次转酒瓶游戏，我得出的结论是：与其梦想着一个我得不到的真爱，不如对整件事变得现实一点。

00:24:54.890 --> 00:25:12.650
<v Howie Chackowicz>One night, after deciding I wanted to have a real girlfriend, I called up identical twin sisters that I liked-- Darlene and Elizabeth. Darlene answered. I told her that I liked her and asked her if she'd like to officially go out with me. She kindly told me that she only liked me as a friend, but she was flattered.
<v Howie Chackowicz>一天晚上，在决定了我想交个真正的女朋友后，我打电话给我喜欢的双胞胎姐妹——达琳和伊丽莎白。达琳接了电话。我告诉她我喜欢她，并问她是否愿意正式和我交往。她好心地告诉我，她只把我当朋友，不过她觉得很荣幸。

00:25:12.650 --> 00:25:22.850
<v Howie Chackowicz>"No problem," I said. "Is Elizabeth home?" She passed me over to her twin, who I made the same offer to, and Elizabeth said, sure. And that was it.
<v Howie Chackowicz>“没问题，”我说。“伊丽莎白在家吗？”她把电话转给了她的双胞胎妹妹，我又向她提出了同样的请求，伊丽莎白说，行啊。事情就这样成了。

00:25:22.850 --> 00:25:33.860
<v Howie Chackowicz>They're identical twins. What's the difference, I figured. We went out for two more months. It was great.
<v Howie Chackowicz>她们是同卵双胞胎。有什么区别呢，我想。我们交往了两个多月。感觉棒极了。

00:25:33.860 --> 00:25:56.330
<v Ira Glass>Howie Chackowicz is the creator, writer, and artist behind Howie Action Comics. Coming up, kids talking kid talk, adults not understanding, but you will, as our special Kids Say the Darndest Things edition of our program continues in a minute from Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>豪伊·查科维奇是“豪伊动作漫画”的创作者、编剧兼画师。接下来，孩子们说着童言稚语，大人们听不懂，但你们会懂的，因为我们节目的特别版“童言无忌”将在芝加哥公共广播电台稍后继续播出。

00:25:56.330 --> 00:26:03.110
<v Ira Glass>This is American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose some topic, bringing you a variety of different kinds of stories on that topic.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周在我们的节目中，我们当然都会选择一个主题，为您带来关于该主题的各种不同类型的故事。

00:26:03.110 --> 00:26:15.930
<v Ira Glass>Today's program, Kid Logic. We wanted an hour filled with stories in which kids employ kid thinking, especially the kid thinking that is perfectly logical but completely wrongheaded. And we have arrived at Act Three of our show.
<v Ira Glass>今天的节目是“儿童逻辑”。我们想要一个充满故事的一小时节目，在这些故事中，孩子们运用儿童式的思考，尤其是那种完全符合逻辑但又错得离谱的儿童思考。现在我们来到了节目的第三幕。

00:26:15.930 --> 00:26:18.980
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, Werewolves in Their Youth.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕：少年狼人。

00:26:18.980 --> 00:26:23.560
<v Ira Glass>We have this excerpt in this act from a short story by Michael Chabon.
<v Ira Glass>我们在这一幕中节选了迈克尔·沙邦的一篇短篇小说。

00:26:23.560 --> 00:26:44.380
<v Michael Chabon>"I had known him as a bulldozer, as a samurai, as an android programmed to kill, as Plastic Man, and Titanium Man, and Matter-Eater Lad, as a Buick Electra, as a Peterbilt truck, and even for a week as the Mackinaw Bridge, but it was as a werewolf that Timothy Stokes finally went too far.
<v Michael Chabon>“我曾把他当作推土机、武士、被编程为杀戮的机器人、塑料人、钛人、吃物小子、别克埃莱克塔轿车、彼得比尔特卡车，甚至有一周把他当作麦金inac大桥，但蒂莫西·斯托克斯最终还是以狼人的身份做得太过分了。

00:26:44.380 --> 00:27:04.600
<v Michael Chabon>I wasn't there when it happened. I was down in the ravine at the edge of the schoolyard, founding a capital for an empire of ants. I had just begun to describe to myself and to the ants the complicated rights sacred to the God whose worship I was imposing on them when I heard the first screams from the playground.
<v Michael Chabon>事情发生时我不在场。我在学校操场边缘的沟壑里，为蚂蚁帝国建立首都。我刚刚开始向自己和蚂蚁们描述那些繁复的权利，这些权利神圣属于我正强迫它们崇拜的神明时，我听到了操场上第一声尖叫。

00:27:04.600 --> 00:27:43.870
<v Michael Chabon>The girls screamed at Timothy the same way every time he came after them-- in unison, and with a trill that sounded almost like delight, as if they were watching the family cat trot past with something bloody in its jaws. I scrambled up the side of the ravine and emerged as Timothy, shoulders hunched, arms outstretched, growled realistically and declared that he was hungry for the throats of puny humans. Timothy said this, or something like it, every time he turned into a werewolf, and I would not have been too concerned if, in the course of his last transformation, he hadn't actually gone and bitten Virginia Pease on the neck.
<v Michael Chabon>女孩子们对蒂莫西尖叫的方式每次都一样——异口同声，带着一种听起来几乎像是喜悦的颤音，就好像她们正在看着家里的猫叼着嘴里血淋淋的东西小跑过去。我顺着沟壑的侧壁爬了上去，看到蒂莫西双肩微驼，双臂张开，发出了逼真的低吼，并宣称他渴望吃掉孱弱人类的喉咙。每次蒂莫西变成狼人时都会说这样的话或类似的话，如果不是在他上次变身的过程中，他真的跑去咬了弗吉尼亚·皮斯的脖子的话，我也不会太担心的。

00:27:43.870 --> 00:28:17.300
<v Michael Chabon>It was common knowledge around school that Virginia's parents had since written a letter to the principal that the next time Timothy Stokes hurt somebody, he was going to be expelled. Timothy was, in our teacher Mrs. Gladfelter's words, one strike away from an out. And there was a widespread, if unarticulated, hope among his classmates, their parents, and all of the teachers at Copland Fork Elementary that one day soon he would provide the authorities with the excuse they needed to pack him off to special school.
<v Michael Chabon>学校里人尽皆知的是，弗吉尼亚的父母此后给校长写了一封信，说如果蒂莫西·斯托克斯下次再伤害任何人，他将被开除。用我们老师格拉德费尔特夫人的话来说，蒂莫西已经“两好球被三振出局”仅一步之遥。科克兰福克小学的同学们、他们的家长以及所有的老师中间，普遍存在着一种（尽管没有明确表达出来的）希望，那就是不久后的某一天，他会给当局提供他们所需要的借口，把他打包送到特殊学校去。

00:28:17.300 --> 00:28:47.730
<v Michael Chabon>I stood there a while above my little city, watching Timothy pursue a snarling, lupine course along the hopscotch crosses. I knew that someone ought to do something to calm him down. But I was the only one in our school who could have any reason to want to save Timothy Stokes from expulsion, and I hated him with all my heart.
<v Michael Chabon>我在我的小城市上方站了一会儿，看着蒂莫西沿着跳方格的十字线进行了一场龇牙咧嘴的狼人式追踪。我知道应该有人做点什么来让他冷静下来。但我是学校里唯一一个有理由想要拯救蒂莫西·斯托克斯免于被开除的人，而我恨他入骨。

00:28:47.730 --> 00:29:10.890
<v Michael Chabon>"I've been cursed for 300 years," he declaimed. He was wearing his standard uniform of white dungarees and a plain white undershirt, even though it was a chilly afternoon in October, and all the rest of us had long since been bundled up for autumn in corduroy and down. "I've been cursed to stalk the night through all eternity," he went on.
<v Michael Chabon>‘我已经受诅咒300年了，’他大声朗诵道。他穿着标准的白色工装裤和一件简单的白色背心，尽管当时是十月一个寒冷的下午，我们其余的人早就穿上了灯芯绒和羽绒服来过冬了。‘我注定要受诅咒在永恒中徘徊在黑夜里，’他继续说道。

00:29:10.890 --> 00:29:26.430
<v Michael Chabon>"I've been searching for prey as lovely as you!" He lunged toward the nearest wall of the cage of girls around him. The girls peeled away from him as though sprayed with a hose, bumped shoulders, clung, shrieking, to each other's sleeves.
<v Michael Chabon>"我一直在搜寻像你这么可爱的猎物！"他猛地扑向周围女生筑起的人墙。女生们像被水管喷到一样从他身边散开，肩膀撞着肩膀，尖叫着紧紧抓住彼此的袖子。

00:29:26.430 --> 00:29:43.560
<v Michael Chabon>Some of them were singing the song we sang about Timothy Stokes. "Timothy Stokes, Timothy Stokes, you're going to the home for crazy folks." And the one singing the loudest was Virginia Pease herself, in her furry black coat and her bright red tights.
<v Michael Chabon>她们当中有些人唱起了我们编的那首关于蒂莫西·斯托克斯的歌。"蒂莫西·斯托克斯，蒂莫西·斯托克斯，你要去疯人收容所啦。"其中唱得最响的正是弗吉尼亚·皮斯本人，她穿着毛茸茸的黑外套和鲜红色的紧身裤。

00:29:43.560 --> 00:30:20.370
<v Michael Chabon>Virginia had blonde hair, and she was the only girl in the fifth grade with pierced ears and painted fingernails, and Timothy Stokes was in love with her. I knew this because the Stokes's lived next door to us, and I was privy to all kinds of secrets about Timothy that I had absolutely no desire to know. I forbade myself with an almost religious severity to show Timothy any kindness or regard. I would never let him sit beside me, at lunch or in class, and if he tried to talk to me on the playground, I ignored him. It was bad enough that I had to live next door to him.
<v Michael Chabon>弗吉尼亚有一头金发，她是五年级里唯一一个打耳洞、涂指甲油的女孩，而蒂莫西·斯托克斯暗恋着她。我知道这一点，因为斯托克斯家就住在我们隔壁，而我对关于蒂莫西的各种秘密可以说是了如指掌，尽管我压根不想知道。我以近乎宗教般的严厉禁止自己对蒂莫西表现出任何友善或关注。我绝不让他坐在我旁边，不管是吃午饭还是在课堂上，如果他在操场上试图跟我说话，我就无视他。能跟他住隔壁就已经够糟糕的了。

00:30:20.370 --> 00:30:39.960
<v Michael Chabon>It was toward Virginia that Timothy now advanced a rattling growl in his throat. She drew back behind her girlfriends, and their screaming now grew less melodious, less purely formal. Timothy crouched down on all fours. He rolled his wild white eyes and took a last look around him.
<v Michael Chabon>蒂莫西现在正朝着弗吉尼亚走去，喉咙里发出咯咯的低吼声。她退到女友们身后，她们的尖叫声此刻变得不再那么悦耳，不再那么流于形式。蒂莫西四肢着地蹲了下来。他转动着发狂的白色眼珠，最后环顾了一下四周。

00:30:39.960 --> 00:31:03.460
<v Michael Chabon>That was when he saw me, halfway across the yellow distance of the soccer field. He was looking at me, I thought, as though he hoped I might have something I wanted to tell him. Instantly I dropped flat on my belly, my heart pounding the way it did when I was spotted trying to spy on a baseball game or a birthday party. I slid down into the ravine backward.
<v Michael Chabon>就在那时他看到了我，隔着足球场黄色的空旷距离。我想，他看着我的眼神，就像是希望我有什么话想对他说一样。我立刻一屁股趴在草地上，心脏狂跳不止，就像当年我因为试图偷看棒球比赛或生日派对而被发现时那样。我倒退着滑进了沟壑里。

00:31:03.460 --> 00:31:34.110
<v Michael Chabon>At first I could hear the girls shouting for Mrs. Gladfelter. And then I heard Mrs. Gladfelter herself, sounding very angry. And the bell sounded the end of recess and everything got very quiet, but I just stayed there in the ravine. I told myself that I didn't feel sorry at all for stupid old Timothy Stokes. But then I would remember the confused look in his eyes as I had abandoned him to his fate, to all the unimaginable things that would be done to him in the fabulous corridors of the special school.
<v Michael Chabon>起接着我听到女孩们在大喊格拉德费尔特太太的名字。然后我听到了格拉德费尔特太太本人的声音，听起来非常生气。接着下课铃响了，四周变得非常安静，但我只是留在沟壑里。我告诉自己，我才不为愚蠢的老蒂莫西·斯托克斯感到难过呢。但随后我就会想起他眼中的那种困惑，因为我把他抛弃了，任由他走向他的命运，走向那些在特殊学校的传奇走廊里将要对做他的所有不可想象的事情。

00:31:34.110 --> 00:32:12.830
<v Michael Chabon>I kept recalling something that I had heard Timothy's mother say to mine just a couple of days earlier. "You know," Althea Stokes had told my mother in that big, sad donkey voice of hers, "your little Paul is Timothy's only friend." I decided to spend the afternoon in the ravine. The sun started down behind the embankment, and the moon, rising early, emerged from the rooftops of the houses somebody was putting up in front of the school. The moon, I noticed, was not quite full.
<v Michael Chabon>我不停地想起几天前我听到蒂莫西的母亲对我妈妈说的一句话。"你知道的，"阿尔西娅·斯托克斯用她那粗哑而悲伤的驴叫般的嗓音对妈妈说，"你们家小保罗是蒂莫西唯一的吸朋友。"我决定在沟壑里度过整个下午。太阳开始落到堤坝后面，月亮早早地升起来，从学校前面正在建造的房子的屋顶上方探出头来。我注意到，月亮还不太圆。

00:32:12.830 --> 00:32:30.020
<v Michael Chabon>I didn't hear the scrape of footsteps until they were just above my head. "Paul," said Mrs. Gladfelter, leaning over the lip of the ravine, hands against her thighs. "Paul Kovel, what on Earth are you doing out here?" "Nothing," I said, "I didn't hear the bell."
<v Michael Chabon>直到脚步声就在我头顶上方响起，我才听到杂音。"保罗，"格拉德费尔特太太说，双手撑着大腿，探身越过沟壑的边缘。"保罗·科维尔，你到底在外面干什么？""没什么，"我说，"我没听到铃声。"

00:32:30.020 --> 00:32:35.180
<v Michael Chabon>"Paul," she said, "now, listen to me. Paul, I need your help." "With what?"
<v Michael Chabon>"保罗，"她说，"现在，听我说。保罗，我需要你的帮助。""帮什么忙？"

00:32:35.180 --> 00:32:55.690
<v Michael Chabon>I didn't think she looked angry, but her face was upside down and it was hard to tell. "With Timothy, Paul. I guess he's just very wound up right now. He's pretending he's a werewolf today. And even though that's fine and we all know how Timothy is sometimes, we have serious things to discuss with him, and we'd like him to stop pretending for just a little while."
<v Michael Chabon>我觉得她看起来并不生气，但她的脸是倒过来的，很难看出来。"关于蒂莫西，保罗。我想他现在情绪非常激动。他今天在假装自己是狼人。尽管这没什么大不了的，我们也都知道蒂莫西有时就是这样，但我们有很严重的事情要跟他谈谈，我们希望他能停下来，哪怕装一会儿普通人也行。"

00:32:55.690 --> 00:33:12.630
<v Michael Chabon>"What if he isn't pretending?" I said. "What if he really is a werewolf?" "Maybe he is, Paul, but if you would just come inside and talk to him for a little bit, I think we might be able to persuade him to change back into Timothy. You're his friend, Paul. I asked him if he'd like to talk to you, and he said yes."
<v Michael Chabon>"如果他不是在装呢？"我说，"如果他真的是个狼人呢？""也许他是，保罗，但如果你肯进来跟他说一会儿话，我想我们也许能说服他变回蒂莫西。你是他的朋友，保罗。我问过他愿不愿意跟你谈谈，他说愿意。"

00:33:12.630 --> 00:33:17.250
<v Michael Chabon>"I'm not his friend, Mrs. Gladfelter. I swear to god. I can't do anything."
<v Michael Chabon>我不是他的朋友，格拉德费尔特太太。我对天发誓。我什么也做不了。

00:33:17.250 --> 00:33:32.490
<v Michael Chabon>"Paul, Timothy is in trouble. He needs your help, and I need your help, too. Now, if you come right this minute and get up out of that dirt, then I'll forget that you didn't come in from recess. If you don't come back inside, I'll have to speak to your mother."
<v Michael Chabon>"保罗，蒂莫西遇上麻烦了。他需要你的帮助，我也需要你的帮助。现在，如果你马上过来，从那个土坑里爬出来，那我就当你没有旷掉课间休息。如果你不进来，我就得去跟你妈妈谈谈了。"

00:33:32.490 --> 00:33:51.240
<v Michael Chabon>She held out her hand. "Now, come on, Paul, please." And so I took her hand and let her pull me out of the ravine and across the deserted playground, aware that in doing so I was merely proving the unspoken corollary that my mother had left hanging the other morning in the air between her and Mrs. Stokes.
<v Michael Chabon>她伸出手。"好了，来吧，保罗，求你了。"于是我握住她的手，任由她把我拉出沟壑，穿过荒凉的操场。我心里清楚，这样做恰恰印证了那天早上我妈妈和斯托克斯太太之间没有明说、却留白在空气中的那个推论。

00:33:51.240 --> 00:34:08.130
<v Michael Chabon>There was a song about me, too, I'm afraid, a popular little number that went "what's that smell-o, Paul Kovel-o, he's a big, fat, hippo Jell-O. He's a snoop. He smells like poop. He smells like tomato beef alphabet soup."
<v Michael Chabon>恐怕连我也有专属的童谣，那是一首挺受欢迎的小调："什么气味噢，保罗·科维尔噢，他是个又大又胖的果冻河马。他是个爱打小报告的家伙。他闻起来像大便。他闻起来像番茄牛肉字母汤。"

00:34:08.130 --> 00:34:40.880
<v Michael Chabon>Timothy Stokes, I knew-- as I followed Mrs. Gladfelter down the long, silent hallway to the office, hating him more and more with each step-- was MY only friend. Timothy was sitting in a corner of the office, trapped in an orange vinyl armchair. There was a Roman numeral three scratched into his left cheek, and his brilliant white shirt and trousers were patterned with a camouflage of grass and dirt and asphalt.
<v Michael Chabon>我深知——当我跟着格拉德费尔特太太走过长长而寂静的走廊去往办公室，每走一步都对他更加憎恨——蒂莫西·斯托克斯才是我的唯一朋友。蒂莫西正坐在办公室的一个角落里，陷在一张橙色乙烯基扶手椅里。他的左脸颊上被抓出一个罗马数字“三”，他那件洁白的衬衫和裤子上沾满了草屑、泥土和柏油，迷彩般斑驳。

00:34:40.880 --> 00:34:53.450
<v Michael Chabon>"Well now, Timothy." Mrs. Gladfelter took me by the shoulders and maneuvered me around her. "Look who I found." "Hey, Timothy," I said. Timothy didn't look up.
<v Michael Chabon>"好了，蒂莫西。"格拉德费尔特太太抓着我的肩膀，把我推到她身前。"看看我找到了谁。""嘿，蒂莫西，"我说。蒂莫西没有抬头。

00:34:53.450 --> 00:35:12.880
<v Michael Chabon>Mrs. Gladfelter gave me a gentle push toward him in the small of my back. "Why don't you sit down, Paul." "No." I didn't want to be left alone with Timothy-- not because I was afraid of him, but because I was afraid that somebody would come into the office and see us sitting there, two matching rejects in matching orange chairs.
<v Michael Chabon>格拉德费尔特太太轻轻推了一下我的后腰，把我朝他推过去。"保罗，你为什么不坐下呢？""不。"我不想和蒂莫西单独呆在一起——不是因为我怕他，而是因为我怕有人会走进办公室看到我们坐在那里，两把一模一样的橙色椅子上坐着两个一模一样的被排挤者。

00:35:12.880 --> 00:35:32.040
<v Michael Chabon>"That's enough now, Paul," said Mr. Buterbaugh, the principal, his friendly smile looking more false than usual. "Sit down." "It's all right," said Mrs. Gladfelter. "You see what you can do about helping Timothy turn back into Timothy. We're just going to give you a little privacy."
<v Michael Chabon>"够了，保罗，"校长巴特博恩先生说，他那友善的微笑看起来比平时更加虚伪。"坐下。""没关系的，"格拉德费尔特太太说，"你看看能不能帮蒂莫西变回蒂莫西。我们这就给你们一点私人空间。"

00:35:32.040 --> 00:35:54.850
<v Michael Chabon>She followed Mr. Buterbaugh into his office and then poked her head back around the door. "I'm going to leave this door open in case you need us, all right?" There were three chairs next to Timothy's. I took the farthest and showed him my back, so that anyone passing by the windows of the office would not be able to conclude that he and I were engaged in any sort of conversation at all.
<v Michael Chabon>她跟着巴特博恩先生走进他的办公室，然后又把头探出门外："我把这扇门开着，以防你们需要我们，好吗？"蒂莫西旁边有三把椅子。我挑了最远的那把坐下，把背对着他，这样任何从办公室窗户经过的人都不会认为他和我在进行任何形式的对话。

00:35:54.850 --> 00:36:08.800
<v Michael Chabon>"Are you expelled?" I said. There was no reply. "Are you, Timothy?" Again he said nothing, and I couldn't stop myself from turning around to look at him. "Timothy, are you expelled?"
<v Michael Chabon>你被开除学籍了吗？我说。没有任何回应。你是吗，蒂莫西？他再次一言不发，我情不自禁地转过身去看着他。蒂莫西，你被开除学籍了吗？

00:36:08.800 --> 00:36:26.450
<v Michael Chabon>"I'm not Timothy, professor," said Timothy, gravely, but not without a certain air of satisfaction. "I'm afraid your precious antidote didn't work." "Come on, Timothy," I said. "cut it out. The moon's not even full today."
<v Michael Chabon>我不是蒂莫西，教授，蒂莫西严肃地说，但并非没有一丝满足感。恐怕你们那个宝贵的解药没起作用。得了吧，蒂莫西，我说。别装了。今天月亮连圆都不是。

00:36:26.450 --> 00:36:40.520
<v Michael Chabon>Now he turned toward me, "where were you," he said, "I was looking for you." "I was in the ditch." "With the ants?" I nodded. "I heard you talking to them before.
<v Michael Chabon>现在他转向我，你去哪儿了，他说，我一直在找你。我在水沟里。跟蚂蚁在一起？我点点头。我刚才听见你跟它们说话了。

00:36:40.520 --> 00:36:53.330
<v Michael Chabon>So, so are you Ant-Man?" "No, dummy." "Why not?" "Because I'm not anybody. You're not anybody either."
<v Michael Chabon>所以，所以你是蚁人？笨蛋。为什么不是？因为我不是任何人。你也不是任何人。

00:36:53.330 --> 00:37:06.860
<v Michael Chabon>We fell silent for a while and just sat there, not looking at each other, kicking at the legs of our chairs. I could hear Mrs. Gladfelter and Mr. Buterbaugh talking softly in his office. Mr. Buterbaugh called her Elizabeth.
<v Michael Chabon>我们沉默了一会儿，只是坐在那里，互不理睬，用脚踢着椅腿。我能听到格拉德费尔特太太和巴特博恩先生在他的办公室里轻声交谈。巴特博恩先生叫她伊丽莎白。

00:37:06.860 --> 00:37:20.720
<v Michael Chabon>The telephone rang. A light flashed twice on the secretary's phone, then held steady. "Thanks for calling back, Dr. Schachter," I heard Mr. Buterbaugh say. "Yes, I'm afraid so."
<v Michael Chabon>电话铃响了。秘书电话上的一盏灯闪了两下，然后常亮起来。"谢谢回电，施特希特医生，"我听到巴特博恩先生说。是的，恐怕是的。

00:37:20.720 --> 00:37:39.360
<v Michael Chabon>I went to see Dr. Schachter a couple of times," I said. "He had Micronauts and the Fembots." "He has Stretch Armstrong, too," said Timothy. "I know." "Why did you go see him," Timothy said. "Did your mother make you?" "Yeah," I said.
<v Michael Chabon>我以前去看过施特希特医生好几次，我说。他有微型人偶和女性机器人。他还有大力士史奇区呢，蒂莫西说。我知道。你为什么去看他，蒂莫西说。你妈妈逼你去的吗？对，我说。

00:37:39.360 --> 00:37:49.630
<v Michael Chabon>"How come?" "I don't know. She said I was having problems with my anger. I don't know. I guess I was mad about my dad and things."
<v Michael Chabon>怎么回事？我不知道。她说我的愤怒情绪有问题。我不知道。我想我是因为我爸爸还有其他事情在生气的。

00:37:49.630 --> 00:38:03.720
<v Michael Chabon>"He had to go to jail," Timothy said, "your dad." "Just for one night." "How come?" "He had too much to drink," I said. "Did you visit him in jail?" Timothy said.
<v Michael Chabon>他不得不去坐牢，蒂莫西说，你爸爸。就一个晚上。怎么回事？他喝了太多酒，我说。你去监狱里探望他了吗？蒂莫西说。

00:38:03.720 --> 00:38:12.930
<v Michael Chabon>"No, stupid. God! You belong in special school, Timothy. I hope they make you eat special food and wear a special helmet or something."
<v Michael Chabon>不，笨蛋。天哪！蒂莫西，你才该去特殊学校。我希望他们逼你吃特制食物，戴特制头盔什么的。

00:38:12.930 --> 00:38:38.980
<v Michael Chabon>I heard the distant slam of the school's front door, and then a pair of hard shoes knocking along the hall. "Here comes your mother," I said. "What kind of special helmet?" said Timothy. "Ant-Man wears a helmet."
<v Michael Chabon>我听到学校正门发出遥远的关门声，接着是一双硬底鞋在走廊里笃笃作响。你妈妈来了，我说。什么特制头盔？蒂莫西说。蚁人戴着头盔。

00:38:38.980 --> 00:38:59.320
<v Michael Chabon>Mrs. Stokes entered the office. She was a tall, thin woman, much older than my mother, with long, gray hair and red, veiny hands. Every morning she made Timothy pancakes for his breakfast, which sounded OK until you found out that she put things in them like carrots and leftover pieces of corn.
<v Michael Chabon>斯托克斯太太走进了办公室。她是一个身材高瘦的女人，比我妈妈大得多，留着长长的灰白头发，双手红肿且血管凸起。每天早上她都给蒂莫西做煎饼当早餐，听起来还不错，直到你发现她在里面加了胡萝卜和吃剩的玉米粒之类的东西。

00:38:59.320 --> 00:39:13.030
<v Michael Chabon>"Oh, hello, Paul," she said in her Eeyore voice. "Mrs. Stokes," said Mrs. Gladfelter, coming out of the principal's office. "It's been kind of a long afternoon for Timothy, I'm afraid."
<v Michael Chabon>哦，你好，保罗，她用她那驴子般的嗓音说。斯托克斯太太，格拉德费尔特太太从校长办公室走出来说道。恐怕对蒂莫西来说，这是一个漫长的下午。

00:39:13.030 --> 00:39:34.800
<v Michael Chabon>"How is Virginia?" said Mrs. Stokes. She still hadn't looked at Timothy. "Oh, she'll be fine, Mr. Buterbaugh said-- just a little shaken up. We sent her home early. Of course," he added, "her parents are going to want to speak to you." "Of course," said Mrs. Stokes. "I'm ready to do whatever you think would be best for Timothy."
<v Michael Chabon>弗吉尼亚怎么样？斯托克斯太太说。她依然没有看蒂莫西一眼。哦，她会没事的，巴特博恩先生说——只是受了点惊吓。我们让她提前回家了。当然，他补充道，她的父母会想跟你谈谈的。当然，斯托克斯太太说。我随时准备好做任何你们认为对蒂莫西最好的事。

00:39:34.800 --> 00:39:44.240
<v Michael Chabon>"I'm not Timothy," said Timothy. "Oh, please, Timmy, stop this nonsense for once." "I'm cursed."
<v Michael Chabon>我不是蒂莫西，蒂莫西说。哦，拜托，蒂米，别再胡闹了。我被诅咒了。

00:39:44.240 --> 00:40:00.540
<v Michael Chabon>He leaned over and brought his face very close to mine. "Tell them about the curse, professor." I looked at Timothy, and for the first time I saw that a thin, dark down of wolfish hair had grown upon his cheek.
<v Michael Chabon>他俯身把脸凑得离我极近。把诅咒的事告诉他们，教授。我看着蒂莫西，第一次发现他的脸颊上长出了一层细密而深色的狼毛。

00:40:00.540 --> 00:40:20.820
<v Michael Chabon>Then I looked at Mr. Buterbaugh and found that he was watching me with an air of earnest expectancy, as though he honestly thought there might be an eternal black magical curse on Timothy and was more than willing to listen to anything I might have to say on the subject. I shrugged. "Are you going to make him go to special school?" I said.
<v Michael Chabon>接着我看着巴特博恩先生，发现他正用一种热切期待的眼神注视着我，仿佛他真的以为蒂莫西身上存在着某种永恒的黑魔法诅咒，并且非常乐意听我讲任何关于这个主题的事情。我耸了耸肩。你们是要送他去特殊学校吗？我说。

00:40:20.820 --> 00:40:33.880
<v Michael Chabon>"All right, Paul, thank you," said Mrs. Gladfelter, "you may go back to class now." "See you later, Timothy," I said. He didn't answer me. He had started to growl again.
<v Michael Chabon>好了，保罗，谢谢你，格拉德费尔特太太说，你现在可以回教室去了。回头见，蒂莫西，我又说。他没有回答我。他又开始低吼了。

00:40:33.880 --> 00:40:55.330
<v Michael Chabon>As I followed the secretary out of the office, I looked back and saw Mr. Buterbaugh and Mrs. Gladfelter and poor old Mrs. Stokes standing in a hopeless circle around Timothy. I thought for a second, and then I turned back toward them and raised an imaginary rifle to my shoulder.
<v Michael Chabon>当我跟着秘书走出办公室时，我回头看到巴特博恩先生、格拉德费尔特太太和可怜的老斯托克斯太太绝望地围成一圈站在蒂莫西身边。我思索了一秒钟，然后转身面向他们，把一支想象中的步枪扛到肩上。

00:40:55.330 --> 00:41:18.600
<v Michael Chabon>"This is a dart gun," I announced. Everyone looked at me, but I was talking to Timothy now. I was almost, but not quite, embarrassed. "It's filled with darts of my special antidote, and I made it stronger than it used to be and it's going to work this time. And also, there's a tranquilizer mixed in."
<v Michael Chabon>这是一把麻醉枪，我宣布。所有人都看着我，但我现在是在对蒂莫西说话。我感到有些尴尬，但又没完全尴尬。里面装满了我的特制解药针剂，我把它做得比以前更浓了，这次绝对管用。而且里面还混合了镇静剂。

00:41:18.600 --> 00:41:34.360
<v Michael Chabon>Timothy looked up and bared his teeth at me, and I took aim right between his eyes. I jerked my hands twice and went fup, fup! Timothy's head snapped back and his eyelids fluttered.
<v Michael Chabon>蒂莫西抬起头冲我龇牙咧嘴，我瞄准他的双眼之间。我的双手猛地一抖，发出“嘭，嘭”的声音！蒂莫西的头猛地向后一仰，眼睑扑扇着。

00:41:34.360 --> 00:41:50.610
<v Michael Chabon>He shook himself all over. He swallowed once. Then he held his hands out before him, as if wondering at their hairless pallor. "It seems to have worked," he said, his voice cool and reasonable and fine.
<v Michael Chabon>他浑身剧烈一抖。吞咽了一口唾沫。接着他把双手伸到身前，仿佛在端详它们那没有毛发覆盖的苍白肤色。"看来起作用了，"他说，声音冷静、理智而完美。

00:41:50.610 --> 00:42:15.060
<v Michael Chabon>Anyone could see he was still playing his endless game, but all the grown ups, Mr. Buterbaugh in particular, looked very pleased with both of us. "Thank you very much, Paul," he said. "I'm not Paul," I said. Everybody laughed but Timothy Stokes."
<v Michael Chabon>任谁都能看出他还在玩着他那没完没了的游戏，但所有大人们，尤其是巴特博恩先生，对我们两个的表现都感到非常满意。"非常感谢你，保罗，"他说。"我不是保罗，"我说。除了蒂莫西·斯托克斯，所有人都笑了。

00:42:15.060 --> 00:42:33.330
<v Ira Glass>An excerpt from Michael Chabon's short story "Werewolves in Their Youth," from the collection of short stories of the same name. Michael Chabon's latest novel is "Telegraph Avenue."
<v Ira Glass>节选自迈克尔·夏邦的短篇小说《青春期狼人》，出自同名短篇小说集。迈克尔·夏邦的最新长篇小说是《电报大街》。

00:42:33.330 --> 00:42:38.130
<v Ira Glass>Act Four, One Brain Shrinks, One Brain Grows.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕，一个大脑萎缩，一个大脑成长。

00:42:38.130 --> 00:42:54.900
<v Ira Glass>Back in 1999, Julie Hill's husband was diagnosed with a rare brain disease. And two years later, when her son Nick was just six years old, he began trying to make sense of what was happening to his dad. Julie gave us this dispatch back then on how her son was thinking about the whole thing at the time.
<v Ira Glass>早在1999年，朱莉·希尔的丈夫被诊断出患有一种罕见的脑部疾病。两年后，当她的儿子尼克只有六岁时，他开始试图理解他爸爸身上到底发生了什么。朱莉当年向我们讲述了她儿子当时是如何看待整件事情的。

00:42:54.900 --> 00:43:09.450
<v Julie Hill>My son is mad at God and hates babies, and I blame The Lion King. I found out about Nick's feelings for babies about a year ago. We'd go to the park and he'd see a baby in a carriage and he'd say, "ew, a baby."
<v Julie Hill>我儿子生上帝的气，而且讨厌婴儿，我怪《狮子王》。我大约在一年前发现了尼克对婴儿的这种感觉。我们会去公园，当他看到婴儿车里的婴儿时就会说：“呃，婴儿。”

00:43:09.450 --> 00:43:23.170
<v Julie Hill>His school has mixed age classes, so there are kids aged three, four, five and six. This fall, our friend's three-year-old little boy named Chance joined Nick's class. We loved Chance and his family. We spend holidays with them.
<v Julie Hill>他的学校是混龄班，所以有三岁、四岁、五岁和六岁的孩子。今年秋天，我们朋友一个三岁的小男孩名叫钱斯，加入了尼克的班级。我们很喜欢钱斯和他的家人。我们一起过节。

00:43:23.170 --> 00:43:36.960
<v Julie Hill>But having Chance in the same class annoyed Nick. Nick taunted him, teasing Chance to tears, telling him, "Go back home, you're too little for school." Nick usually isn't mean like that. We couldn't figure it out.
<v Julie Hill>但班里多了个钱斯让尼克感到很烦躁。尼克嘲弄他，把钱斯逗哭，对他说：“回你家去吧，你太小了，不适合上学。”尼克平时不是那种刻薄的孩子。我们怎么也想不通。

00:43:36.960 --> 00:43:54.190
<v Julie Hill>Then one night at bedtime, Nick asked me, "mom, is Chance a baby?" I explained that Chance is much younger, but technically he wasn't a baby. Nick was quiet a while, and then he asked me if babies replace dying people.
<v Julie Hill>后来有一天晚上快睡觉时，尼克问我：“妈妈，钱斯是婴儿吗？”我解释说钱斯小得多，但严格来说他不是婴儿。尼克沉默了一会儿，接着问我婴儿是不是用来替代那些快死的人的。

00:43:54.190 --> 00:44:10.570
<v Julie Hill>Finally, I got it. You see, in The Lion King, the hero's father dies, and the big lesson of the film is that it's one big cycle. Some might die, but others are born. Some get to eat, and others are eaten. It's all part of the great, great circle of life.
<v Julie Hill>终于，我明白了。你看，在《狮子王》里，主角的父亲死了，而这部电影的核心教训就是生命是一个巨大的循环。有些人会死，但另一些人会出生。有些动物有得吃，有些则被吃掉。这一切都是伟大宏伟的生命轮回的一部分。

00:44:10.570 --> 00:44:32.000
<v Julie Hill>So if you're a kid, you think if you could stop babies from being born, you'd stop people from dying-- you'd stop the circle. Nick thought if there were no babies, then maybe his dad wouldn't die. So to Nick, Chance was a threat to his dad's very existence.
<v Julie Hill>所以如果你是个孩子，你就会觉得只要你能阻止婴儿出生，就能阻止人们死亡——就能阻止这个循环。尼克认为如果没有婴儿，那么也许他爸爸就不会死。因此在尼克看来，钱斯简直是他爸爸生存下去的巨大威胁。

00:44:32.000 --> 00:44:50.100
<v Julie Hill>If you ask my husband's college friends about him, they'll tell you Doug was a comedian. Not that he really ever made money at it. Doug was an advertising copywriter, actually, but dabbled in improv and stand-up and recorded humor. Back in the '80s when answering machines were new, we sold tapes of wacky outgoing messages.
<v Julie Hill>如果你去问我丈夫大学时的朋友，他们会告诉你道格是个喜剧演员。倒不是说他真的靠这个赚过钱。其实道格是个广告文案撰稿人，但他也涉足即兴表演、单口喜剧，还录制过幽默录音。在80年代答录机刚兴起的时候，我们还卖过那种搞笑留言磁带。

00:44:50.100 --> 00:44:58.640
<v Doug Hill>Hi. Sorry I can't come to the phone right now. I'm in the bathtub listening to my radio. So if you want to, just leave your message and your name at the tone.
<v Doug Hill>嗨。抱歉我现在不能接电话。我正在浴缸里听收音机。所以如果你愿意的话，听到“哔”的一声后请留下你的留言和姓名。

00:44:58.640 --> 00:44:59.790
<v Doug Hill>[KNOCKING]
<v Doug Hill>[敲门声]

00:44:59.790 --> 00:45:03.110
<v Doug Hill>Oh, there's somebody at the door. Hey, whoops! Ah!
<v Doug Hill>噢，有人敲门。嘿，哎呀！啊！

00:45:03.110 --> 00:45:04.500
<v Doug Hill>[BUZZING AND SPLASHING]
<v Doug Hill>[嗡嗡声和水花声]

00:45:04.500 --> 00:45:09.150
<v Doug Hill>[SCREAMING]
<v Doug Hill>[尖叫声]

00:45:09.150 --> 00:45:22.740
<v Julie Hill>OK, so that's not that funny. Still, I miss him so much. He was my best friend, and I'd take that stupid silliness any day. This is a recent voicemail from Doug.
<v Julie Hill>好吧，那其实没那么好笑。不过，我还是非常想念他。他是我最好的朋友，我随时都愿意接受他那种傻乎乎的幼稚。这是道格最近留下的语音信箱。

00:45:22.740 --> 00:45:24.450
<v Automated Voice>Sent Monday.
<v Automated Voice>星期一发送。

00:45:24.450 --> 00:45:40.860
<v Doug Hill>Eric Clapton, Max somebody, Columbus, gir-- Columbus, girls up-- or whatever. OK. OK, bye.
<v Doug Hill>埃里克·克莱普顿，某个叫麦克斯的人，哥伦布，女孩——哥伦布，女孩们——随便吧。好吧。好吧，再见。

00:45:40.860 --> 00:46:02.300
<v Julie Hill>His brain's shrinking. My husband's brain is shrinking. The results of Doug's PET scan showed that he had frontal lobe dementia, a rare degenerative and ultimately fatal disease that slowly atrophies the brain. Its progression is similar to that of a baby's development, only backward.
<v Julie Hill>他的大脑正在萎缩。我丈夫的大脑正在萎缩。道格的的正电子发射断层扫描（PET）结果显示，他患有额颞叶痴呆症，这是一种罕见的退行性且最终致命的疾病，会导致大脑缓慢萎缩。它的发展过程类似于婴儿的发育，只是过程相反。

00:46:02.300 --> 00:46:28.500
<v Julie Hill>The doctor said Doug, my 38-year-old husband who ran marathons and insisted on organic vegetables, had two to 10 years to live. During this time, he'd slowly, slowly lose his ability to work, drive, reason, feel emotions, count, go to the bathroom, walk, talk, swallow, and breathe.
<v Julie Hill>医生说，道格——我38岁的丈夫，跑过马拉松，坚持吃有机蔬菜——只有两到十年的寿命了。在这期间，他将慢慢地、慢慢地失去工作、开车、推理、感受情绪、数数、上厕所、走路、说话、吞咽和呼吸的能力。

00:46:28.500 --> 00:46:48.540
<v Julie Hill>Since then, Nick has tried to make logical sense of this mostly illogical disease, and he does this by looking for patterns to help sort it all out. These come up all the time. For instance, before he got sick, Doug drove most of the time. But as he got sicker, he could only sit in the passenger seat.
<v Julie Hill>从那以后，尼克一直试图从逻辑上理解这种大多不合逻辑的疾病，他通过寻找规律来把这一切理出头绪。这些规律随时都会出现。例如，在他生病之前，大部分时间都是道格开车。但随着病情加重，他只能坐在副驾驶座上。

00:46:48.540 --> 00:47:09.550
<v Julie Hill>About six months after we took the car keys away from Doug, my friend Tim came in from San Francisco. Tim hopped into the passenger seat. Nick sat in the back in his booster. He stared at Tim sitting just in front of him and then asked, "Tim, do you have a brain disease?"
<v Julie Hill>在我们拿走道格的车钥匙大约六个月后，我的朋友蒂姆从旧金山赶来。蒂姆跳上了副驾驶座。尼克坐在后座的儿童增高椅上。他盯着坐在他正前方的蒂姆，然后问：“蒂姆，你也有脑部疾病吗？”

00:47:09.550 --> 00:47:29.080
<v Julie Hill>I'm not sure when it stopped making sense for Doug to live at home. Maybe it was the day he took a cab downtown and was missing until late that night. Maybe it was the day he went outside with nothing on but shaving cream. Or maybe it was the day he let a homeless person into our house. But at some point it was just too much-- especially too much for Nick to see.
<v Julie Hill>我不太确定是从什么时候开始，道格继续在家生活变得不再合理。也许是那天他坐出租车去了市中心，直到那天深夜才失踪。也许是那天他身上除了剃须泡沫什么都没穿就跑到了户外。又或者，那天他放了一个无家可归的人进我们家。但在某个时刻，这一切实在太过了——尤其是对尼克来说，看到这些实在太过了。

00:47:29.080 --> 00:47:48.010
<v Julie Hill>Doug moved into long-term care in late March. After Doug moved, though, Nick's stress changed. Two weeks later, Nick came out of his bedroom crying. He missed his dad. He just wanted him to get better and to come home.
<v Julie Hill>道格在三月下旬搬进了长期护理机构。不过，在道格搬走后，尼克的压力发生了变化。两周后，尼克哭着走出卧室。他想念他的爸爸。他只是希望爸爸能好起来并回家。

00:47:48.010 --> 00:47:59.000
<v Julie Hill>I had what I thought was a great idea-- to call Doug up. I wanted to prove his dad was alive, that contact was possible. We went through the rigmarole to get Doug on the phone.
<v Julie Hill>我当时觉得这是个绝妙的主意——给道格打电话。我想证明他爸爸还活着，联系是可能的。我们经历了一番繁琐的程序才终于联系上了道格。

00:47:59.000 --> 00:48:07.180
<v Julie Hill>"I love you, daddy," Nick said. "I love you," Doug repeated. "I miss you, daddy." "I miss you," Doug said.
<v Julie Hill>“我爱你，爸爸，”尼克说。“我爱你，”道格重复道。“我想你，爸爸。”“我想你，”道格说。

00:48:07.180 --> 00:48:28.930
<v Julie Hill>Nick started to tell Doug about his day, how he played outside and how he skinned his knee. Then I heard the recording of the operator. "If you'd like to place a call, please hang up." Doug had hung up on Nick.
<v Julie Hill>尼克开始向道格讲述他的一天，他如何在外面玩耍以及他的膝盖是怎么擦伤的。然后我听到了接线员的录音：“如果您想拨打电话，请挂断。”道格挂断了尼克的电话。

00:48:28.930 --> 00:48:41.330
<v Julie Hill>The way Nick makes sense of all this can catch me off-guard. His conclusions are perfectly logical. And sometimes the only way to address his fears is to use the same logic he does, with the same assumptions.
<v Julie Hill>尼克理解这一切的方式常常让我措手不及。他的结论完全合乎逻辑。有时，解决他恐惧的唯一方法就是采用与他相同的逻辑和相同的假设。

00:48:41.330 --> 00:49:02.530
<v Julie Hill>For example, last Mother's Day weekend, Nick and I had a session with Cory, his therapist. Mostly in these sessions I talk, and Cory helps me figure out appropriate responses to some of Nick's really big questions. But this time, Nick interrupted. He wanted to know what would happen to him if I died.
<v Julie Hill>例如，去年母亲节周末，尼克和我与他的治疗师科里进行了一次面谈。在这些面谈中，通常是我在说，科里帮我找出对尼克一些真正重大问题的恰当回应。但这一次，尼克打断了我们。他想知道如果我死了，他会怎么样。

00:49:02.530 --> 00:49:25.030
<v Julie Hill>We gave the answer that clinically was supposed to satisfy him-- that I'm healthy, that I eat right, that I exercise, not to worry. And for a preschooler, that was supposed to do. But he called us on the carpet. "Daddy did all those things, too." Nick wasn't supposed to make that leap. The books say so.
<v Julie Hill>我们给出了临床上本应让他满意的答案——我很健康，我饮食健康，我锻炼身体，不用担心。对于一个学龄前儿童来说，这本该足够了。但他却反驳了我们。“爸爸也做了所有这些事情。”尼克本不该得出这个推论。书上是这么说的。

00:49:25.030 --> 00:49:34.400
<v Julie Hill>And then he said, "Mommy, if you died, I'd be in the house all by myself. Who'd make dinner for me? How would I get to school?"
<v Julie Hill>然后他说：“妈妈，如果你死了，我就会一个人呆在家里。谁来给我做晚饭？我怎么去学校？”

00:49:34.400 --> 00:49:48.860
<v Julie Hill>We explained that my parents would take care of him, and then he jumped to yet another level. "But it takes a long time to get to Chicago from Ohio where Grandma and Grandpa live. Who would take care of me until they got here?"
<v Julie Hill>我们解释说我的父母会照顾他，然后他又跳到了另一个层面上。“但从俄亥俄州——外公外婆住的地方——到芝加哥要花很长时间。在我外公外婆到那儿之前，谁来照顾我？”

00:49:48.860 --> 00:50:23.380
<v Julie Hill>Nick is a planner. Having a plan seems to help him. So in a moment of inspiration, Cory took out a picture of Nick from my wallet. On it she wrote specific instructions that, in an emergency, call his babysitter Amber to take care of Nick while his grandparents drove from Ohio. Just having that little card in my wallet, that little plan, allowed him to let that fear go.
<v Julie Hill>尼克是个有规划的人。有个计划似乎对能帮助他。所以在灵感闪现的一瞬间，科里从我的钱包里拿出了尼克的一张照片。她在上面写下了具体指令：如果在紧急情况下，打电话给他的保姆琥珀来照顾尼克，同时外公外婆从俄亥俄州开车赶来。钱包里仅仅装着那张小卡片、那个小计划，就让他放下了这种恐惧。

00:50:23.380 --> 00:51:00.950
<v Julie Hill>We don't know what caused Doug to get sick. It could be a toxin, it could be a long-forgotten head injury, it could be genetics. I try to pack that information away next to the Christmas ornaments, but one look at Nick and I get scared. Since no celebrity has this disease, there's very little money devoted for research, and so I'm doing this story for Nick. Not only so that people will understand this little life now, but maybe someone will take notice and start working on preventing this terrible disease before my kindergartner turns 30.
<v Julie Hill>我们不知道是什么导致道格生病的。可能是某种毒素，可能是很久以前被遗忘的头部受伤，也可能是基因。我试着把这些信息像圣诞饰品一样收起来，但只要看尼克一眼，我就会感到害怕。由于没有名人患这种疾病，用于研究的资金非常少，所以我正在为尼克讲述这个故事。不仅是为了让人现在能理解这种短暂的小生活，也许还会有人注意到并开始着手研究，在我这个上幼儿园的儿子满30岁之前，阻止这种可怕的疾病。

00:51:00.950 --> 00:51:15.730
<v Julie Hill>You might think it's all dark and dreary at our house. It's not. Actually, we're doing OK. This month, Nick graduated from kindergarten and he got his first bike without training wheels. And he's over his circle of life theory.
<v Julie Hill>你可能会觉得我们的生活一片黑暗和阴郁。其实并没有。实际上，我们过得还不错。这个月，尼克幼儿园毕业了，他还得到了他的第一辆没有辅助轮的自行车。而且他已经不再纠结他的人生循环理论了。

00:51:15.730 --> 00:51:36.070
<v Julie Hill>At night I stay in Nick's bed until he falls asleep. It's a bad habit his father formed right after he was diagnosed, like was trying to squeeze one last cuddle out of the day. Since I've taken over, it's become the time to talk about Doug, about everything. So for the past two weeks, I've brought a tape recorder into Nick's bed.
<v Julie Hill>晚上我留在尼克的床上，直到他睡着。这是他父亲在他被诊断出病情后不久养成的一个坏习惯，就像是试图从一天中榨出最后的一个拥抱。自从我接手以来，这就成了谈论道格、谈论一切的时间。所以在过去的两个星期里，我把一台录音机带进了尼克的床上。

00:51:36.070 --> 00:51:41.860
<v Julie Hill>Tell me about Daddy, when daddy had to move. What do you think about that?
<v Julie Hill>跟我说说爸爸吧，当爸爸不得不搬走的时候。你对此有什么看法？

00:51:41.860 --> 00:51:44.470
<v Nick Hill>Really sad.
<v Nick Hill>真的很伤心。

00:51:44.470 --> 00:51:47.290
<v Julie Hill>What makes you sad about that?
<v Julie Hill>是什么让你对此感到伤心？

00:51:47.290 --> 00:51:53.910
<v Nick Hill>I don't have a dad to read the comics to me.
<v Nick Hill>我没有爸爸给我读漫画了。

00:51:53.910 --> 00:51:57.700
<v Julie Hill>Sometimes think, I wish daddy could come home?
<v Julie Hill>有时你会想，希望爸爸能回家吗？

00:51:57.700 --> 00:52:00.740
<v Nick Hill>Yeah.
<v Nick Hill>会。

00:52:00.740 --> 00:52:05.450
<v Julie Hill>But he's not going to be able to, is he?
<v Julie Hill>但他不可能回来了，对吧？

00:52:05.450 --> 00:52:09.300
<v Nick Hill>But what if he died?
<v Nick Hill>但如果他死了呢？

00:52:09.300 --> 00:52:15.700
<v Julie Hill>How would you feel?
<v Julie Hill>你会有什么感觉？

00:52:15.700 --> 00:52:29.170
<v Nick Hill>The saddest person in the whole wide world.
<v Nick Hill>全世界最伤心的人。

00:52:29.170 --> 00:52:52.990
<v Julie Hill>One night, Nick asked me if I cried when his namesake, my grandfather, died. I said, "yes, I did." "Do you still cry about him?" I told him that I didn't, that right after someone dies, we tend to cry a lot, but after a while we stop crying and just feel lucky that we got to know and love that person so, so much.
<v Julie Hill>一天晚上，尼克问我，当他的同名长辈、我的祖父去世时，我有没有哭。我说：“是的，我哭了。”“你现在还会为他哭吗？”我告诉他我不会了，人在刚去世后，我们往往会哭得很伤心，但过了一段时间后，我们就不再哭了，只会觉得自己能认识并那么、那么深爱那个人，是件很幸运的事。

00:52:52.990 --> 00:53:13.150
<v Julie Hill>Nick thought about that for a while, taking it all in. And then he said, "I know why you stopped crying. You decided that if you ever had a boy that you love so, so much that you'd name him Nick so you'd remember your grandpa, and that's why you stopped crying."
<v Julie Hill>尼克想了一会儿，把这一切都听了进去。然后他说：“我知道你为什么不哭了。你决定了，如果你以后生了一个你那么、那么深爱的男孩，你就要给他起名叫尼克，这样你就会记住你的爷爷，这就是你不再哭的原因。”

00:53:13.150 --> 00:53:36.670
<v Julie Hill>I think he's looking to me now for how to grieve, for what the next step is going to be. That it's OK to be sad and cry, and then it's OK when you finally stop crying, too.
<v Julie Hill>我想他现在正看着我，想知道该如何悲伤，下一步该怎么做。难过和哭泣是没有问题的，最后终于不再哭泣了，这也是没有问题的。

00:53:36.670 --> 00:53:59.050
<v Nick Hill>Uh, I'm tired. Let's get off the phone. Now, good night, everybody in America. I'm sleepy. Let's go to bed. Let's turn that off and go to bed.
<v Nick Hill>呃，我累了。我们挂电话吧。现在，晚安，全美国的每个人。我困了。我们去睡觉吧。把那个关掉，去睡觉。

00:53:59.050 --> 00:54:13.670
<v Ira Glass>Julie Hill and her son in Chicago.
<v Ira Glass>朱莉·希尔和她的儿子在芝加哥。

00:54:13.670 --> 00:54:22.910
<v Ira Glass>Our program was produced today by Jonathan Goldstein and myself with Alex Blumberg, Wendy Dorr, and Starlee Kine. Our senior producer is Julie Snyder. Production help from Todd Bachmann and Paul McCarthy, Seth Lind, and Tommy Andres.
<v Ira Glass>我们今天的节目由乔纳森·戈德斯坦和我制作，参与制作的还有亚历克斯·布隆伯格、温迪·多尔和斯塔莉·凯恩。我们的资深制作人是朱莉·斯奈德。托德·巴赫曼、保罗·麦卡锡、赛斯·林德和汤米·安德雷斯提供了制作协助。

00:54:22.910 --> 00:54:43.280
<v Ira Glass>Special thanks today to Vivian Paley, Bill Ayers, Bernardine Dohrn, Michael Cohen of [INAUDIBLE] Research and Consulting, Elaine Evans, Brett Berk, Julie Rigby, Jennifer Fields, Jack Hitt and Noah Miller. Our website where you can get our absolutely free podcast or listen to old episodes of our show, or see video from our new TV show, www.thisamericanlife.org.
<v Ira Glass>今天我们要特别感谢薇薇安·帕利、比尔·艾尔斯、伯纳丁·多恩、[听不清]研究与咨询公司的迈克尔·科恩、伊莱恩·埃文斯、布雷特·伯克、朱莉·里格比、詹妮弗·菲尔兹、杰克·希特和诺亚·米勒。我们的网址是 www.thisamericanlife.org，您可以在上面获取我们完全免费的播客，收听我们节目的往期节目，或者观看我们新电视节目的视频。

